/* Chapter 13 — canonical topics, questions, flashcards and exam prompts. */
IFL_DATA.registerChapter({"number":13,"title":"Some Accessory Contracts","part":"part-iii","partRoman":"III","pages":[347,356],"minutes":40,"estimates":{"f1":40,"f2":45},"difficulty":"Intermediate","difficultyAlt":"intermediate","learningObjectives":[{"id":"lo-ch13-1","text":"Define Wakalah, its types and rules, and explain its use in Islamic banking including Wakalatul Istismar.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch13-2","text":"Define Tawarruq, distinguish it from Bai‘ al ‘Inah, and evaluate the juristic views and the cautions against its extensive use.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch13-3","text":"Explain Tawarruq for inter-bank liquidity management and its safeguards.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch13-4","text":"Define Ju‘alah and explain its parties, subject matter, reward, execution, parallel contracts and banking products.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch13-5","text":"Describe Bai‘ al Istijrar (supply contracts) and its conditions.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch13-6","text":"Define Wakalah (agency) and identify which acts can and cannot be delegated to an agent.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch13-7","text":"Explain Wakalatul Istismar and how Islamic banks charge fees for fund-management agency services.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch13-8","text":"Explain Tawarruq's structure, its distinction from the prohibited Bai' al 'Inah, and why scholars urge caution about its widespread use.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch13-9","text":"Describe how genuine Tawarruq arrangements are used for interbank liquidity management, and the structuring pitfalls that can invalidate them.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch13-10","text":"Define Ju'alah, distinguish it from Ijarah, and explain why it suits tasks with an uncertain outcome or unspecifiable work.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch13-11","text":"Walk through the practical bank process for Ju'alah, including Parallel Ju'alah, and identify real financial products built on it.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch13-12","text":"Explain Bai' al Istijrar (supply contract) and the conditions under which repeat-sale arrangements are Shari'ah-compliant.","origin":["f2"]}],"why":"These ‘accessory’ contracts appear inside nearly every Islamic banking product: agency in Murabaha and Ijarah, Ju‘alah in debt recovery, Tawarruq in liquidity management and personal finance. Examiners like the Tawarruq–‘Inah distinction and the conditions under which organised Tawarruq becomes invalid.","whyExpanded":"These accessory contracts - Wakalah, Tawarruq, Ju'alah, and Istijrar - are the connective tissue that makes the major financing modes (Murabaha, Salam, Istisna'a, Ijarah, Diminishing Musharakah) actually work in practice. Nearly every Islamic bank transaction involves an agency relationship somewhere, and understanding Tawarruq's genuine structure versus its abuse, and Ju'alah's unique suitability for uncertain-outcome tasks, is essential for recognizing both legitimate innovation and disguised interest in modern Islamic finance.","overview":"The chapter covers four ancillary contracts. Wakalah (agency) with its types, rules and fund-management variant (Wakalatul Istismar); Tawarruq with the juristic views, invalidating structures and scholarly cautions, and its use for liquidity; Ju‘alah (reward for a result) with its parties, reward, execution, parallel contracts and products; and Bai‘ al Istijrar (repeat supply sale).","summary":"Chapter 13 explains contracts that support the major modes. Wakalah (agency) requires a known, lawful and representable task; the agent acts as a trustee with due care, avoids undisclosed conflicts and cannot delegate without consent; goods he buys pass directly to the principal; it may be paid or unpaid and underlies Murabaha, Ijarah, Salam, Istisna‘a, DM, L/Cs and fund management — Wakalatul Istismar paying a pre-agreed fee irrespective of results. Tawarruq — buying on credit and selling spot to a third party — is permissible in the preferred view of the four schools and AAOIFI, unlike ‘Inah; it becomes invalid when the client acts as the bank's agent to buy and sell to himself or when sale-agency is stipulated, and scholars (Kahf, Siddiqi) warn that extensive or consumer use undermines Islamic banking, so it should be limited to unavoidable corporate liquidity needs with strict monitoring. Ju‘alah pays a known reward only upon achieving a specified result, suits uncertain work such as debt recovery, needs no counter-acceptance for public offers, may be paralleled, and supports collection, financing-facilitation and brokerage products. Istijrar is a repeat-supply arrangement with relaxed price rules absent Gharar. (Chapter synthesis — the chapter has no separate summary section.)","summaryExpanded":"Chapter 13 covers the accessory contracts that make Islamic finance's major modes operationally possible. Wakalah (agency) underlies nearly every Islamic bank transaction, from MPO to Ijarah to fund management (Wakalatul Istismar), with clear rules on what can be delegated and how an agent's actions bind the principal. Tawarruq generates liquidity through a credit-purchase-then-cash-resale structure, permissible across all four schools when resold to a genuine third party (never back to the original seller, which would be prohibited Bai' al 'Inah) - though scholars strongly caution against its widespread, routine use. Ju'alah's reward-for-result structure uniquely suits tasks with unspecifiable work or uncertain outcomes (debt recovery, lost-property recovery), distinguishing it sharply from Ijarah's requirement of precisely specified work. Bai' al Istijrar rounds out the chapter as a flexible, ordinary repeat-supply arrangement rather than a distinct mode.","summarySection":null,"takeaways":["Wakil = trustee; ownership passes directly to principal.","Wakalatul Istismar: fixed fee regardless of results, disclosed in prospectus.","Tawarruq ≠ ‘Inah: sale to a third party.","Client as bank's agent to buy and self-sell → invalid Tawarruq.","Limit Tawarruq to unavoidable corporate liquidity needs.","Ju‘alah: no result, no reward; revocation after work starts → reasonable wage.","Parallel Ju‘alah contracts must be independent.","Istijrar: repeat sale; Murabaha consignments each need offer and acceptance.","Wakalah (agency) can cover almost any transferable business act (sale, hire, borrowing, guarantee) but never acts that must be performed personally (prayer, testimony, oath); an agent's action is deemed the principal's own action.","Wakalatul Istismar lets Islamic banks earn a fixed fee for fund management, regardless of the fund's actual profit or loss - a structural contrast to Mudarabah's profit-sharing model.","Tawarruq (buy on credit, sell for cash to a third party) is permissible per the preferred view of all four Fiqh schools, but reselling back to the original seller constitutes prohibited Bai' al 'Inah - and scholars strongly caution against Tawarruq's widespread, routine use, especially for individual consumers.","Ju'alah's defining feature is that payment depends strictly on realizing the specified result, not on the work performed - making it uniquely suited to tasks (lost property, debt recovery) where Ijarah's precise-work-specification requirement cannot be met.","Banks commonly use Parallel Ju'alah (taking on a customer's job, then independently subcontracting the actual work to a third-party expert) and real products built on Ju'alah include debt collection, financing-facilitation, and brokerage.","Bai' al Istijrar is not a distinct mode but a flexible, ordinary repeat-supply arrangement; if structured via a specific mode like Murabaha, that mode's full conditions (including per-consignment offer and acceptance) still apply."],"takeawayOrigins":["f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2"],"checklist":["Can you list five types of Wakalah and the agent's duties?","Can you explain Wakalatul Istismar vs Mudarabah?","Can you distinguish Tawarruq from ‘Inah and state the juristic views?","Can you identify invalid organised Tawarruq structures?","Can you describe interbank Tawarruq steps?","Can you explain Ju‘alah rules on reward and termination?","Can you draw the Figure 13.1 process?","Can you explain Istijrar?"],"topics":[{"id":"t13.1","section":"13.1","title":"Introduction: Ancillary Contracts","pages":[347,347],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["wakalah","tawarruq","jualah"],"intuition":"Which small contracts make the big modes work?","simpleExplanation":"Besides major contracts (Shirkah, Bai‘, Ijarah), Islamic banks use ancillary contracts inside or alongside them: Wakalah (agency), Ju‘alah (service against reward), Tawarruq (generating cash through purchase and sale, rooted in Murabaha but now a separate mode) and Istijrar (repeat sale). Hawalah, Kafalah and Bai‘ al Dayn are covered in other chapters.","academicExplanation":["Wakalah and Ju‘alah are discussed as ancillary contracts; Tawarruq has become a separate mode used with other major modes; Istijrar is a repeat-sale arrangement under major modes.","The chapter gathers contracts that rarely stand alone in Islamic banking but make the major modes work. Wakalah (agency) runs through Murabaha, Salam, Istisna‘a, Ijarah, Diminishing Musharakah, L/Cs, bill collection, fund management and securitisation. Ju‘alah (a reward for achieving a result) covers work too uncertain to be specified for Ijarah. Tawarruq (monetisation) is essentially a Murabaha followed by a spot sale, but it has become a separate product. Istijrar is a framework for repeated sales. Hawalah, Kafalah and Bai‘ al Dayn are dealt with in other chapters (p. 347)."],"examExplanation":"Accessory contracts: Wakalah, Ju‘alah, Tawarruq, Istijrar (Hawalah, Kafalah, Bai‘ al Dayn elsewhere).","keyPoints":["Ancillary contracts support the major modes.","Hawalah, Kafalah and Bai‘ al Dayn are covered in their own chapters, not here (p. 347)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Accessory contracts inside one product","kind":"practice","text":"In a car Ijarah the bank appoints the client as agent to buy the car (Wakalah), takes a guarantee (Kafalah) and a pledge of a deposit (Rahn). None of these is the main mode, but the product depends on them (p. 347)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t13.2","t13.3","t13.4","t13.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which of these is discussed in Chapter 13 as an accessory contract?","options":["Musharakah","Ju‘alah","Salam","Takaful"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 347."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch13-t1"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch13-t1","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"13.1","sectionTitle":"Introduction","title":"Accessory Contracts in Islamic Finance","covers":["t13.1"],"overview":"Introduces the chapter's scope: Wakalah and Ju'alah as the two primary accessory contracts covered in depth, with Tawarruq and Istijrar also discussed, while Hawalah, Kafalah and Bai' al Dayn are addressed elsewhere in the book.","simpleExplanation":"Beyond the major contracts (partnership, sale, leasing), Islamic finance relies on smaller 'helper' contracts that get used inside or alongside the big ones. This chapter covers agency (Wakalah), a get-cash-through-buying-and-selling technique (Tawarruq), a pay-for-results contract (Ju'alah), and a repeat-supply arrangement (Istijrar).","academicExplanation":"Major contracts used by Islamic financial institutions - Shirkah, Bai', Ijarah, and similar - have been discussed separately in prior chapters. This chapter covers ANCILLARY contracts used as part of, or alongside, those major contracts. Two are discussed in depth: Wakalah (agency) and Ju'alah (rendering a service against reward). Tawarruq (monetization/generating cash through purchase-sale activity) essentially carries the concept of Murabaha in some form, but has become a distinct mode Islamic banks use alongside major modes, warranting separate discussion. Istijrar, a repeat-sale arrangement that can occur under various major modes, is discussed briefly. Other subcontracts - Hawalah (assignment of debt), Kafalah (guarantee), and Bai' al Dayn (sale of debt) - are covered in their relevant chapters elsewhere in the book, not repeated here.","examExplanation":"Chapter scope: Wakalah (agency) and Ju'alah (reward-for-result) discussed in depth; Tawarruq (cash-generation via purchase/sale, Murabaha-derived) discussed as a distinct mode; Istijrar (repeat-sale arrangement) discussed briefly. Hawalah, Kafalah, Bai' al Dayn are covered elsewhere in the book, not here.","keyPoints":["This chapter covers ancillary/accessory contracts used within or alongside the major Islamic finance contracts (Shirkah, Bai', Ijarah) covered in prior chapters.","Two contracts are discussed in depth: Wakalah (agency) and Ju'alah (rendering a service for a reward).","Tawarruq essentially derives from the concept of Murabaha but has become a distinct financing mode, warranting separate treatment.","Istijrar (a repeat-sale arrangement) is covered briefly; Hawalah, Kafalah, and Bai' al Dayn are covered in other chapters of the book, not repeated here."],"definitions":[],"conditions":[],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":[],"distinctions":[],"relatedConcepts":["Wakalah","Ju'alah","Tawarruq","Istijrar"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.1","pages":[347]}}]},{"id":"t13.2","section":"13.2","title":"Wakalah (Agency)","pages":[347,349],"tier":"core","concepts":["wakalah"],"intuition":"When a client buys goods ‘on behalf of the bank’, whose goods are they?","simpleExplanation":"Wakalah means looking after or acting for others; Tawkeel is appointing someone to take charge. The Wakil discharges his responsibility as a trustee (Amanah). The subject matter must be known, lawful and capable of representation (not prayer, fasting, testimony or oaths). The agent follows instructions with due care, cannot delegate without consent, must avoid conflicts of interest, and his acts are the principal's. Wakalah ends by agreement, termination, completion, destruction of the subject matter, death or loss of capacity.","academicExplanation":["Legitimacy: the Prophet (pbuh) delegated the purchase of a goat to ‘Urwah al Barqi; Ali and other Companions delegated business.","Types: Wakil-bil-Kusoomah (disputes), Wakil-bil-Taqazi al Dayn (receiving debt), Wakil-bil-Qabaza al Dayn (possession of debt), Wakil-bil-Bai‘ (sale), Wakil-bil-Shira (purchase).","For purchase agencies, genus, kind, quality and attributes must be specified; agency in prohibited acts (theft, usurpation, Riba business) is not allowed. Permissible fields include sale, hire, lending, assignment, guarantee, pledge, gifts, bailment, payments, marriage, divorce and litigation.","Specific vs general agency: a general agency to buy goods as and when asked; a specific agency to sell a particular asset at a given price — the job must still be clearly defined.","Rights of delivery, claiming price, options of defect and inspection attach to the agent, who can be sued for non-performance; marriage, divorce and settlement of murder attach to the principal.","The agent cannot sell his own property to the principal without disclosure; banks should include a negligence liability clause.","Preferred view (Shafi‘i, Hanbali): goods bought by an agent pass directly from seller to principal. An unauthorised actor (Fuduli) creates contracts valid subject to the principal's ratification (preferred view).","Uses: Murabaha, Salam, Istisna‘a, Ijarah, Diminishing Musharakah, L/Cs, bill collection, fund management and securitisation. It may be with or without fee — banks usually pay clients nothing as agents but charge fees for agency services they provide."],"examExplanation":"Wakalah = agency; Wakil is trustee. Five types. Subject matter known, lawful, representable. Agent: follow instructions, due care, no sub-delegation without consent, disclose conflicts. Ownership passes directly to principal. Fuduli valid on ratification. Ends: agreement, termination, completion, destruction, death/incapacity.","keyPoints":["Acts requiring personal performance (prayer, oath, testimony) cannot be delegated.","Agency may be paid or unpaid.","Used across Murabaha, Ijarah, Salam, Istisna‘a, DM.","The Wakil discharges his responsibility as a trustee does with an Amanah (p. 347).","Rights such as delivery, claiming the price and exercising options of defect or inspection attach to the agent. Marriage, divorce and settlement of murder attach explicitly to the principal (p. 348).","Wakalah ends by mutual agreement, unilateral termination, discharge of the obligation, destruction of the subject matter, death or loss of legal capacity (p. 348)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Wakalah","definition":"Agency: appointing another to act on one's behalf; the agent (Wakil) acts as a trustee."},{"term":"Fuduli","definition":"A person who acts for another without authority; the contract is valid subject to the principal's ratification (preferred view)."},{"term":"Tawkeel","definition":"Appointing someone to take charge of something, or delegating a job to another person; derived from the same root as Wakalah."}],"examples":[{"title":"Purchase of a goat","kind":"textbook","text":"The Prophet (pbuh) delegated the purchase of a goat to the Companion ‘Urwah al Barqi. Ali and other Companions also delegated their business to others (p. 347)."},{"title":"Agent selling his own goods","kind":"textbook","text":"An agent buying for his principal cannot supply his own property without fully disclosing that it belongs to him, because he must avoid conflicts of interest (p. 348)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Goods bought by an agent first become the agent’s property and then pass to the principal.","right":"On the preferred view (especially of the Shafi‘is and Hanbalis), ownership passes directly from the seller to the principal (p. 348)."},{"wrong":"A contract concluded by a Fuduli (unauthorised person) is void.","right":"The preferred view is that it is valid subject to the principal’s ratification (p. 348)."}],"relatedTopics":["t13.2.1","t13.2.2","t9.8.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Under the preferred (Shafi‘i/Hanbali) view, goods bought by an agent for the principal:","options":["Belong first to the agent","Pass directly from the seller to the principal","Remain with the seller","Must be resold"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 348."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch13-t2"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch13-t2","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"13.2-13.2.1","sectionTitle":"Wakalah (Agency) / Types of Wakalah","title":"Wakalah (Agency): Definition, Types, and General Rules","covers":["t13.2","t13.2.1"],"overview":"Defines Wakalah, catalogs its recognized types, and details what acts can and cannot be delegated to an agent, plus the agent's duties, liabilities, and how a Wakalah contract ends.","simpleExplanation":"Wakalah just means appointing someone to act on your behalf - like a trustee looking after your interests. It's fully backed by the Prophet's (pbuh) own practice (he once delegated buying a goat to a companion). You can delegate almost any normal business task (buying, selling, lending, guaranteeing, payments) but NOT personal religious/legal acts you must do yourself (like praying, giving testimony, or taking an oath). The agent must follow instructions carefully, can't secretly sell his own property to the person he's representing without disclosing it, and generally can't pass the job to someone else without permission. If the agent messes up through negligence, he can be held liable.","academicExplanation":"'Wakalah' literally means looking after, taking custody, or applying skill/remedy on behalf of others - the word 'Tawkeel' derives from it, meaning to appoint someone to take charge or delegate a job. Wakalah is also a RESPONSIBILITY - a Wakil (agent) must discharge his duty the way a trustee discharges Amanah. Five recognized types: Wakil-bil-Kusoomah (taking up disputes/cases on the principal's behalf), Wakil-bil-Taqazi al Dayn (receiving debt), Wakil-bil-Qabaza al Dayn (possession of debt), Wakil-bil-Bai' (trading agency), Wakil-bil-Shira (purchase agency). Agency is proven by Shari'ah texts - the Prophet (pbuh) himself delegated goat-purchasing to Companion 'Urwah al Barqi; Caliph Ali and other Companions delegated business to others. The subject matter/act to be performed must be known/defined - for a purchase agency, the genus, kind, quality, and other necessary attributes of the commodity must be specified. Agency is NOT permissible for acts prohibited by Shari'ah (theft, property usurpation, Riba-based business) or acts of disobedience. An agency-eligible act must ADMIT REPRESENTATION - agency for prayer, fasting, giving evidence, or taking an oath is not permissible, since these must be performed by the principal personally (e.g. an eyewitness must give testimony himself, not delegate it). Acts for which agency CAN be invoked: sale/purchase, letting/hiring, borrowing/lending, debt assignment, guarantee, pledge, gifts, bailment, payments, marriage/divorce, litigation/relinquishment, and admission/acknowledgement of rights. An agency contract may be SPECIFIC (e.g. selling a particular asset at a given price/instruction) or GENERAL (e.g. purchasing certain kinds of goods as and when asked) - even in a general contract, the job's nature must be clearly defined to avoid disputes. Rights/liabilities attributed to the agent include: delivery of subject matter, claiming price, exercising Khiyar al 'Aib/inspection options, and returning goods on reclaim - the agent is responsible for making payment/receiving goods on the principal's behalf, can demand price from a purchaser (in a sale), and can be SUED for non-performance per the agency agreement. Certain contracts, however, are attributed explicitly to the PRINCIPAL only - marriage, divorce, and murder settlement. The agent must perform per the principal's instructions with due care/skill, cannot delegate to another person without the principal's consent, and must avoid conflicts of interest (e.g. cannot sell his own property to the principal without fully disclosing his ownership). Agency can generate THIRD-PARTY liabilities - banks appointing agents should ensure good faith/due diligence, with a contractual clause imposing liability for negligence. An agent's action on the principal's behalf is deemed the PRINCIPAL's own action; per the preferred view (especially Shafi'i and Hanbali), ownership of a commodity purchased by an agent transfers DIRECTLY from seller to principal, without first passing through the agent's ownership. A person acting WITHOUT proper authority is termed Fuduli - the preferred view is that such unauthorized contracts are VALID subject to the principal's subsequent RATIFICATION. A Wakalah contract ends via: mutual agreement, unilateral termination, discharge of obligation, destruction of the subject matter, or death/loss of legal capacity. Wakalah is used by IFIs across almost all modes (Murabaha, Salam, Istisna'a, Ijarah, Diminishing Musharakah) and activities (L/Cs, bill payment/collection, fund management, securitization). Wakalah can be COMMUTATIVE (fee-based) or NONCOMMUTATIVE (no fee) - Islamic banks mostly do NOT pay clients acting as their purchase/sale agents, but normally DO charge fees for agency services they themselves render on clients' behalf (e.g. Wakalatul Istismar fund management).","examExplanation":"Wakalah = agency, a trust-based responsibility. 5 types: Wakil-bil-Kusoomah (disputes), Wakil-bil-Taqazi/Qabaza al Dayn (debt receipt/possession), Wakil-bil-Bai' (trading), Wakil-bil-Shira (purchase). Agency requires the task to be known/defined and must ADMIT REPRESENTATION - personal religious/legal acts (prayer, testimony, oath) CANNOT be delegated; business acts (sale, hire, borrowing, guarantee, pledge, payments) CAN. Specific agency (one asset/task) vs general agency (recurring category). Agent's action = principal's action (per Shafi'i/Hanbali, ownership transfers DIRECTLY seller-to-principal, bypassing the agent). Fuduli (unauthorized agent) contracts are VALID subject to principal's ratification (preferred view). Wakalah ends via: mutual agreement, unilateral termination, discharge, destruction of subject matter, or death/incapacity. Used across virtually all IFI modes; can be fee-based (commutative) or free (noncommutative) - banks typically charge for THEIR agency services rendered, but don't pay clients acting as the bank's purchase/sale agent.","keyPoints":["An agency-eligible act must 'admit representation' - personal religious/legal acts (prayer, fasting, giving testimony, taking an oath) cannot be delegated to an agent, since they must be performed by the principal personally.","An agency contract can be specific (a particular asset/task) or general (a recurring category of tasks), but the job's nature must always be clearly defined to avoid disputes.","Per the preferred view (especially Shafi'i and Hanbali), ownership of a commodity purchased by an agent transfers DIRECTLY from seller to principal, without first passing through the agent's own ownership.","A Fuduli (a person acting without proper authority) - the preferred view holds such unauthorized contracts are VALID, subject to subsequent ratification by the principal.","A Wakalah contract ends via mutual agreement, unilateral termination, discharge of obligation, destruction of the subject matter, or death/loss of legal capacity.","Wakalah can be commutative (fee-based) or noncommutative (free); Islamic banks typically do NOT pay clients acting as their purchase/sale agents, but DO charge fees for agency services they themselves render to clients (e.g. Wakalatul Istismar)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Wakalah","definition":"Agency - appointing someone (a Wakil) to look after, take custody of, or apply skill on behalf of another, discharged with the same trust-based responsibility as Amanah."},{"term":"Fuduli","definition":"A person who undertakes a job/contract on behalf of another without proper authority; per the preferred juristic view, such contracts are valid subject to the principal's subsequent ratification."}],"conditions":["Agency is not permissible for acts prohibited by Shari'ah (theft, property usurpation, Riba-based business) or for acts that must be performed personally by the principal (prayer, fasting, giving testimony, taking an oath).","The subject matter or act to be performed by the agent must be known/defined - for a purchase agency, the genus, kind, quality, and necessary attributes of the commodity must be specified.","The agent cannot delegate his task to another person without the principal's consent, and must avoid conflicts of interest, such as selling his own property to the principal without full disclosure."],"principles":["An action performed by an agent on the principal's behalf is deemed to be an action of the principal himself."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"The Prophet's Delegated Goat Purchase","kind":"textbook","text":"The Prophet (pbuh) himself delegated the job of purchasing a goat for him to a Companion named 'Urwah al Barqi - cited as foundational textual evidence for the permissibility of agency (Wakalah)."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think any task can be delegated via Wakalah. Acts that must be performed personally by the principal (prayer, fasting, giving testimony, taking an oath) explicitly cannot be delegated - only acts that 'admit representation' qualify."],"distinctions":["Acts attributed to the agent (delivery, price claims, inspection rights - the agent can be sued for non-performance) vs acts attributed explicitly to the principal only (marriage, divorce, murder settlement)."],"relatedConcepts":["Amanah","Wakalatul Istismar","Murabaha to Purchase Orderer"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2-13.2.1","pages":[347,348,349]},"transactionSteps":[{"step":"Principal Identifies Need","description":"A principal (an individual, bank, or investor) has a task or transaction requiring representation - such as purchasing a commodity, managing funds, or conducting a Murabaha/Salam/Istisna'a/Ijarah leg - and decides to appoint an agent (Wakil) rather than act personally."},{"step":"Scope and Subject Matter Defined","description":"Principal and agent agree the subject matter of the agency: for a purchase agency, the genus, kind, quality, and other necessary attributes of the commodity must be specified; the agency may be specific (one particular asset/task) or general (a recurring category), but its nature must always be clearly defined to avoid disputes."},{"step":"Fee Arrangement Agreed (if Commutative)","description":"The parties settle whether the Wakalah is commutative (fee-based) or noncommutative (gratuitous); if fee-based, the fee - fixed as a lump sum or as a percentage (e.g. of investment amount or net asset value, as in Wakalatul Istismar) - is agreed and disclosed before the agent begins acting."},{"step":"Agent Acts Within Scope","description":"The agent performs the specified act (buying, selling, collecting or paying debt, litigation, etc.) with due care and skill, per the principal's instructions, without delegating the task to another without consent, and without any conflict of interest, such as selling his own property to the principal without disclosure."},{"step":"Legal Effect Attaches to the Principal","description":"The agent's action is deemed the principal's own action; per the preferred view (especially Shafi'i and Hanbali), ownership of any commodity purchased by the agent transfers directly from the seller to the principal, without first passing through the agent's own ownership."},{"step":"Principal Bears the Outcome","description":"The principal bears the profit, loss, or risk of the underlying transaction - this is what distinguishes Wakalah from a risk-sharing contract like Mudarabah or Musharakah; the agent himself is liable only for negligence, misconduct, or acting outside the agreed scope."},{"step":"Fee Paid and Contract Ends","description":"The agent receives any agreed fee for services rendered, and the Wakalah contract terminates by mutual agreement, unilateral termination, discharge of the obligation, destruction of the subject matter, or death or loss of legal capacity of either party."}]}],"table":{"caption":"Types of Wakalah (Section 13.2.1)","head":["Type","Purpose"],"rows":[["Wakil-bil-Kusoomah","Disputes/cases for the principal"],["Wakil-bil-Taqazi al Dayn","Receiving debt"],["Wakil-bil-Qabaza al Dayn","Possession of debt"],["Wakil-bil-Bai‘","Selling"],["Wakil-bil-Shira","Purchasing"]]}},{"id":"t13.2.1","section":"13.2.1","title":"Types and Rules of Wakalah in Practice","pages":[347,349],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["wakalah"],"intuition":"What should a bank write into an agency agreement with a client?","simpleExplanation":"Banks appointing agents should define the job clearly (even in general agency), require good faith and due diligence, prohibit undisclosed self-dealing, and include a clause making the agent liable for negligence, because agency can create third-party liabilities.","academicExplanation":["The agent is responsible for payment and receipt of goods on the principal's behalf and may be sued for non-performance; he must exercise due care and skill and cannot entrust the work to another without consent.","The book lists five types of agent: Wakil-bil-Kusoomah (disputes and litigation), Wakil-bil-Taqazi al Dayn (receiving a debt), Wakil-bil-Qabaza al Dayn (taking possession of a debt), Wakil-bil-Bai‘ (selling) and Wakil-bil-Shira (buying). An agency may be general or specific. A bank that appoints an agent to buy certain kinds of goods whenever asked creates a general agency. Asking an agent to sell a particular asset at a set price creates a specific one. In either case the job must be clearly defined. For a purchase, that means the genus, kind, quality and other attributes of the goods (p. 348). The act must also admit representation. Prayer, fasting, giving evidence and taking an oath must be done personally, and there can be no agency for prohibited acts such as theft, usurpation or Riba-based business (p. 348).","In practice, Islamic banks usually pay no fee to clients who buy or sell as their agents. The banks do charge fees when they act as agents for clients, for example in fund management (p. 349). Because an agency can create liabilities to third parties, banks should make sure agents act in good faith and with due diligence. The contract may make the agent liable for negligence (p. 348)."],"examExplanation":"Bank practice: clear job definition; negligence clause; no delegation without consent; disclose conflicts.","keyPoints":["Third-party liabilities can arise from agency.","Wakalah may be for a fee (commutative) or free (non-commutative) (p. 349)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"General vs specific agency","kind":"textbook","text":"General: a bank appoints an agent to buy certain goods whenever it asks. Specific: a bank asks an agent to sell a particular asset at a given price (p. 348)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"An eyewitness may appoint an agent to testify on his behalf.","right":"Testimony, like prayer, fasting and oaths, must be given in person and cannot be delegated (p. 348)."}],"relatedTopics":["t13.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Can an agent sell his own property to the principal?","options":["Never","Only with full disclosure that it is his","Always, secretly","Only at a loss"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 348."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"table":{"caption":"Types of Wakil (p. 347)","head":["Type","Scope"],"rows":[["Wakil-bil-Kusoomah","Disputes and cases for the principal"],["Wakil-bil-Taqazi al Dayn","Receiving a debt"],["Wakil-bil-Qabaza al Dayn","Taking possession of a debt"],["Wakil-bil-Bai‘","Selling"],["Wakil-bil-Shira","Buying"]]}},{"id":"t13.2.2","section":"13.2.2","title":"Wakalatul Istismar (Investment Agency)","pages":[349,349],"tier":"core","concepts":["wakalah","fund-management"],"intuition":"How does a fund manager's fee differ from a Mudarib's profit share?","simpleExplanation":"IFIs can manage investors' funds as agents for a pre-agreed fee irrespective of profit or loss — a lump sum or a periodic percentage of investment or net asset value (e.g. 2% or 3% of NAV annually) — with all profits and losses passed to investors. The fee basis must be fixed before launch, typically disclosed in the prospectus, which subscribers are presumed to accept.","academicExplanation":["The fee method must be determined before the fund launches; disclosure in the prospectus makes it agreed by all subscribers.","Under Wakalatul Istismar the bank manages investors’ funds as their agent. Its pre-agreed fee does not depend on profit or loss, and all profit and loss go to the investors. The fee can be a lump sum, or a periodic percentage of the amount invested or of net asset value, e.g. 2–3 % of NAV at each year-end. The method must be fixed before the fund is launched. In practice it is disclosed in the prospectus, and every subscriber is taken to have accepted it (p. 349)."],"examExplanation":"Wakalatul Istismar: fixed pre-agreed fee (lump sum or % of investment/NAV) regardless of results; P&L to investors; fee basis disclosed in prospectus before launch.","keyPoints":["Fee is independent of performance, unlike a Mudarib's share."],"definitions":[{"term":"Wakalatul Istismar","definition":"Investment agency: management of investors' funds for a pre-agreed fee, with profit and loss belonging to the investors."}],"examples":[{"title":"Fee on NAV","kind":"practice","text":"A fund with a year-end NAV of 500 million and a disclosed fee of 2 % of NAV pays the manager 10 million, whether the fund made a profit or a loss that year (based on p. 349)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"The Wakalah fee basis may be decided after the fund has been running for a year.","right":"One method must be settled before launch, normally by disclosure in the prospectus (p. 349)."}],"relatedTopics":["t12.4.2","t16.4.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In Wakalatul Istismar, the manager is paid:","options":["A share of profit only","A pre-agreed fee irrespective of profit or loss","Interest on funds","Nothing"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 349."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch13-t3"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch13-t3","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"13.2.2","sectionTitle":"Wakalatul Istismar","title":"Wakalatul Istismar: Fund Management Agency","covers":["t13.2.2"],"overview":"Explains how Islamic banks manage investor funds on an agency (fee-for-service) basis, earning a pre-agreed fee regardless of the portfolio's actual profit or loss.","simpleExplanation":"Instead of sharing profit like in a Mudarabah, a bank can simply act as a paid agent managing investor money - charging a flat fee (either a lump sum or a percentage of the fund's value each year), no matter how the investments actually perform. This fee structure has to be decided and clearly disclosed BEFORE the fund launches, typically in the fund's prospectus, and everyone who invests is presumed to accept those disclosed terms.","academicExplanation":"Islamic financial institutions can manage investor funds on the basis of Wakalatul Istismar (investment agency services). For their services, banks can charge a PRE-AGREED fee, IRRESPECTIVE of the relevant portfolio's profit or loss - this fee can be a lump sum, or a monthly/annual remuneration as a percentage of investment amount or net asset value (NAV) (e.g. an agreement that management gets 2-3% of the fund's NAV at each financial year end). It is necessary to determine the specific fee-calculation method BEFORE the fund launches - practically done by disclosing the management fee basis in the fund's prospectus; whoever subscribes to the fund is presumed to agree with the prospectus's terms, so the fee-payment manner is taken as agreed upon by all subscribers.","examExplanation":"Wakalatul Istismar = agency-based fund management; bank earns a PRE-AGREED FIXED fee (lump sum, or % of investment/NAV annually/monthly) REGARDLESS of actual profit/loss - unlike Mudarabah's profit-sharing structure. Fee method must be determined and disclosed (typically in the prospectus) BEFORE fund launch; subscribers are presumed to accept the prospectus's disclosed terms.","keyPoints":["Wakalatul Istismar is an agency-based fund management structure where the bank earns a pre-agreed fee (lump sum, or a percentage of investment amount/net asset value) regardless of the portfolio's actual profit or loss.","This contrasts with Mudarabah's profit-sharing model - under Wakalatul Istismar, the bank's compensation is fixed by fee terms, not tied to the fund's performance.","The specific fee-calculation method must be determined and disclosed BEFORE the fund launches, typically via the fund's prospectus.","Subscribers to the fund are presumed to agree to the disclosed fee terms in the prospectus, so the fee-payment method is treated as agreed upon by all subscribers."],"definitions":[{"term":"Wakalatul Istismar","definition":"Investment agency services - an arrangement where an Islamic financial institution manages investor funds for a pre-agreed fee (fixed or percentage-based), regardless of the fund's actual profit or loss."}],"conditions":["The fee-calculation method for Wakalatul Istismar must be determined and disclosed to investors before the fund is launched, typically via the fund's prospectus."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"2-3% of Net Asset Value","kind":"textbook","text":"A fund's prospectus may stipulate that the management will receive 2% or 3% of the net asset value of the fund at the end of every financial year, regardless of whether the fund's underlying investments were profitable that year."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes confuse Wakalatul Istismar with Mudarabah-based fund management. In Mudarabah, the manager's return is a share of actual profit (and none if there's a loss); in Wakalatul Istismar, the manager earns a FIXED fee regardless of the fund's profit or loss."],"distinctions":["Wakalatul Istismar (fixed fee, independent of fund performance) vs Mudarabah-based fund management (profit-share, contingent on actual fund performance)."],"relatedConcepts":["Mudarabah","Wakalah","Net Asset Value"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2.2","pages":[349]},"calculations":[{"title":"Practice Example — generated for learning: Wakalatul Istismar Fixed Management Fee","formula":"Management fee = Agreed fee rate x Net Asset Value (NAV) of the fund, paid irrespective of the fund's actual profit or loss","inputs":[{"label":"Fund's Net Asset Value (NAV) at financial year end","value":"$50,000,000"},{"label":"Agreed management fee rate (per the fund's prospectus)","value":"2% of NAV per annum"},{"label":"Fund's actual investment performance this year","value":"-3% (a loss on the underlying portfolio)"}],"steps":["Per the prospectus, the bank (as Wakil) is entitled to a pre-agreed fixed annual fee of 2% of NAV, determined and disclosed before the fund's launch, regardless of whether the underlying investments profit or lose money.","Fund NAV at year end = $50,000,000.","Management fee = 2% x $50,000,000 = $1,000,000.","The fund's investments actually lost 3% in value this year - a genuine investment loss borne entirely by the investors, not the bank - but the bank's Wakalah fee is unaffected, since it is computed on NAV, not on performance."],"result":"The bank earns a $1,000,000 management fee for the year - the full 2% of NAV - despite the fund's -3% investment performance.","interpretation":"This illustrates the structural contrast with Mudarabah-based fund management drawn in the text: under Wakalatul Istismar, the bank's compensation is a pre-agreed fee (lump sum, or a percentage of investment amount/NAV) fixed and disclosed before the fund launches, and it does not vary with the fund's actual profit or loss. All investment risk and return, positive or negative, flows entirely to the investors, while the bank is compensated purely for its agency/management service - a mechanism similar to how a Takaful operator's Wakalah fee is charged for managing the participants' risk fund.","generated":true,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2.2","pages":[349]}}]}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Wakalatul Istismar","b":"Mudarabah","text":"The Wakil earns a fixed fee regardless of results; the Mudarib earns only a share of actual profit and nothing if there is no profit."}]},{"id":"t13.3","section":"13.3","title":"Tawarruq","pages":[349,351],"tier":"core","concepts":["tawarruq","inah"],"intuition":"If someone buys on credit only to sell for cash, is that different from borrowing at interest?","simpleExplanation":"Tawarruq is buying on credit and selling at spot to a third party to obtain cash. Selling back to the original seller is ‘Inah and not compatible per the majority. The preferred view in all four schools is that genuine Tawarruq is permissible (though Qard al Hasan is preferable); AAOIFI agrees. But it becomes invalid if the bank appoints the client its agent to buy and then sell to himself, or if agency to sell is stipulated in the sale. Scholars advise it be limited to unavoidable corporate liquidity needs.","academicExplanation":["Hanbali and Shafi‘i jurists mainly discuss it and distinguish it from ‘Inah: the Mutawarriq sells to a third party, whereas in ‘Inah the buyer resells to the original seller.","Views: majority Hanbali — permissible (Ibn Taymiyah and Ibn Qayyim held it impermissible); Maliki — no major problem, a way to avoid Riba; some later Hanafis — ‘Inah, abominable; majority Hanafi follow Ibnul-Hummam — valid if sold in the market.","Valid: the bank buys a liquid commodity and sells to the Mutawarriq, who sells in the market. Invalid: the bank appoints the Mutawarriq its agent to buy and then sells to himself (interdependent, no bank possession or risk). Valid but not advisable: agent only for purchase, then a separate sale by the bank with offer and acceptance.","If the Mutawarriq appoints the bank as agent to sell and this is stipulated in the sale — invalid; if arranged after an unconditional sale — valid but not advisable.","Through commodity exchanges where goods never move and only brokers act, it is vulnerable to violation of sale conditions. Some banks use shares, Ijarah Sukuk or ‘bundles of assets’; extensive use should be avoided.","Monzer Kahf: limited to unavoidable corporate liquidity needs; for individual consumers it must be completely out. Nejatullah Siddiqi: the client comes wanting cash and leaves with cash after signing papers. Careless wide use may create systemic risk."],"examExplanation":"Tawarruq = buy on credit, sell spot to third party for cash. ‘Inah = resale to original seller (invalid). Preferred view in four schools + AAOIFI: permissible. Invalid if client is bank's agent to buy and sell to himself, or sale-agency stipulated. Cautions: Kahf, Siddiqi; limit to corporate liquidity.","keyPoints":["Grey area; widely used in personal finance and credit cards.","Qard al Hasan is preferable.","Commodity-exchange Tawarruq vulnerable to paper-only trades.","Some banks run Tawarruq through shares, Ijarah Sukuk and “bundles of assets” with a majority of real assets. These may be permissible on legal grounds but should not be used extensively (p. 350)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Tawarruq","definition":"Buying a commodity on credit and selling it at spot to a third party to obtain cash."},{"term":"Mutawarriq","definition":"The person who obtains liquidity through Tawarruq."},{"term":"Bai‘ al ‘Inah","definition":"Sale and buy-back: the buyer resells the commodity to the original seller at a different price — a device for Riba."},{"term":"Mutawarriq","definition":"The person who obtains liquidity through Tawarruq by buying on credit and selling for cash."}],"examples":[{"title":"Tawarruq vs ‘Inah","kind":"textbook","text":"A buys copper from bank B on credit for 110 and sells it to an unrelated trader for 100 cash: Tawarruq. If A sold it back to B for 100, it would be ‘Inah — the same cash flows, but prohibited by the majority (pp. 349–350)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"The Maliki school, being strict on ‘Inah, rejects Tawarruq.","right":"The Malikis see no major problem in Tawarruq and regard it as a way to avoid Riba (p. 349)."},{"wrong":"If every formal condition of sale is met, heavy use of Tawarruq raises no concern.","right":"The transaction may be valid, but its extensive use is still problematic and could create systemic risk (p. 350)."}],"relatedTopics":["t13.3.1","t6.11","t9.9.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"The bank appoints the customer as its agent to buy a commodity and then to sell it to himself. The Tawarruq is:","options":["Valid and advisable","Invalid — interdependent transactions without bank possession or risk","Valid only for corporates","Required by AAOIFI"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 350."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch13-t4"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch13-t4","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"13.3","sectionTitle":"Tawarruq","title":"Tawarruq: Concept, Permissibility, and the Bai' al 'Inah Distinction","covers":["t13.3","t13.3.1"],"overview":"Defines Tawarruq as a liquidity-generation technique, distinguishes it from the prohibited Bai' al 'Inah, surveys the four-school scholarly debate, and warns of structuring pitfalls that can invalidate genuine Tawarruq.","simpleExplanation":"Tawarruq means buying something on credit and immediately reselling it for cash - not because you actually want the item, but because you need liquidity. The critical rule: you must resell it to a DIFFERENT third party, not back to whoever you bought it from - selling back to the original seller turns it into 'Inah (a disguised loan), which is prohibited. All four major schools of Islamic law, after debate, have settled on Tawarruq being permissible when done this way - though a straightforward interest-free loan (Qard al Hasan) is still considered preferable when available. There are also ways this can go wrong even when following the basic rule: if a bank makes the customer its OWN agent to both buy AND sell (to himself), that's invalid since the two transactions become interdependent and the bank never truly takes ownership/risk.","academicExplanation":"Tawarruq means buying on credit and selling at spot value to obtain CASH - the underlying transaction itself is not the buyer's actual need; he simply wants liquidity, achieved by purchasing a commodity on credit and immediately reselling it for cash. Selling to a THIRD PARTY is Shari'ah-acceptable; selling BACK to the original credit-seller is NOT Shari'ah-compatible per the majority view. Despite being a grey area, Tawarruq is used by many Islamic banks for liquidity management and financing (especially personal financing and credit cards). Classical treatment is mainly by Hanbali and Shafi'e jurists, who distinguish it from Bai' al 'Inah: the difference is that the 'Mutawarriq' (the liquidity-seeker) sells the commodity to a THIRD PARTY, while in 'Inah, the buyer resells to the SAME seller from whom he bought, with a price differential. Hanbali and Shafi'e jurists generally allow Tawarruq - two versions are reported from Imam Ahmad Ibn Hanbal, with the MAJORITY of Hanbali jurists preferring the permissible version (though Ibn Taymiyah and Ibn Qayyim held it impermissible). Maliki jurists (very strict about 'Inah) see no major problem with Tawarruq, viewing it as a way to AVOID Riba. Some LATER Hanafi jurists held Tawarruq equals 'Inah (thus abominable), but the MAJORITY of Hanafi jurists prefer Ibnul-Hummam's view: 'Inah is restricted specifically to reselling back to the ORIGINAL seller; if sold in the open market (to a third party), the transaction is valid and permissible - though Qard al Hasan (interest-free lending) remains MORE PREFERABLE where available. CONCLUSION: the preferred view across ALL FOUR Fiqh schools is that Tawarruq is permissible. AAOIFI likewise holds: selling the commodity back (directly OR indirectly through any agent) to the original deferred-payment seller is INVALID; selling to a genuine third party is acceptable. However, the ruling changes if OTHER elements infiltrate the transaction: (1) If a bank purchases a brisk-market commodity and sells it to the Mutawarriq, who THEN independently sells it in the market himself, there's no Shari'ah problem. (2) BUT if the bank appoints the Mutawarriq as ITS agent to purchase the commodity on the bank's behalf, and THEN sell it to HIMSELF (the same person, wearing two hats), the transaction is INVALID - the purchase and sale become interdependent, and the bank never genuinely takes possession/business risk. (3) HOWEVER, if the bank appoints him as agent ONLY for the purchase (on the bank's behalf), and once purchased, the BANK ITSELF sells it to him through a SEPARATE contract with proper offer/acceptance, the transaction is valid - though NOT ADVISABLE (a structuring pitfall risk even when technically valid). (4) If a Mutawarriq appoints the bank as HIS agent to sell a commodity he would purchase from the bank for Tawarruq, and this agency is STIPULATED AS A CONDITION in the sale contract itself, the transaction is INVALID; but if the agency was NOT a sale-contract condition and was arranged AFTER an unconditional sale, it's valid - though still not advisable. (5) Tawarruq conducted through national/international COMMODITY EXCHANGES (where only brokers perform agency services and goods never actually move/transfer ownership from seller to buyer) is VULNERABLE to Shari'ah rule violations, since several valid-sale conditions may be lacking. Some Islamic banks conduct Tawarruq via joint stock company shares, Ijarah Sukuk (tangible assets and services), and even 'bundles of assets' (real assets plus cash/receivables, with real assets in the majority) - though legally permissible, EXTENSIVE use of such financing needs to be AVOIDED. Shari'ah scholars/experts advise Tawarruq's practice should be of LIMITED use for the corporate sector's unavoidable liquidity needs only. Monzer Kahf: 'For individual consumers it must be completely out of the Islamic banking practices.' Nejatullah Siddiqi's critique: 'The client approaches the Islamic bank with a wish to have cash and a collateral and comes back with the desired cash after signing a number of papers' - the author warns this WIDE, CARELESS use of Tawarruq creates SYSTEMIC RISK for the nascent finance industry. Even when all valid-sale conditions are properly observed, extensive/widespread use remains problematic.","examExplanation":"Tawarruq = buy on credit, sell for spot cash to a DIFFERENT third party (to generate liquidity). Selling BACK to the original credit-seller = Bai' al 'Inah (prohibited). All FOUR schools' preferred view: Tawarruq IS permissible (Hanbali/Shafi'e/Maliki generally allow; majority Hanafi view per Ibnul-Hummam: 'Inah is restricted to reselling to the SAME seller; third-party resale is valid) - though Qard al Hasan is still MORE preferable when available. AAOIFI: selling back to the original seller (directly OR via agent) = invalid; third-party sale = acceptable. STRUCTURING PITFALLS (even when the basic rule is followed): bank appointing the Mutawarriq as agent to BOTH buy AND sell to himself = INVALID (interdependent transactions, no genuine bank risk-bearing); bank appointing him ONLY as purchase agent, then bank sells to him via a SEPARATE contract = valid but NOT ADVISABLE; agency stipulated as a sale-contract CONDITION = invalid; agency arranged AFTER unconditional sale = valid but not advisable. Commodity-exchange Tawarruq (no real ownership transfer) = vulnerable to Shari'ah violations. Scholarly caution: limited use for corporate liquidity ONLY; Kahf says NEVER for individual consumers; Siddiqi warns of paper-signing exercises; widespread use = systemic risk.","keyPoints":["Tawarruq = buying on credit and immediately reselling for cash, purely to generate liquidity (not because the buyer actually needs the commodity) - reselling to a THIRD PARTY is acceptable; reselling BACK to the original credit-seller is Bai' al 'Inah, prohibited by the majority view.","The preferred view across ALL FOUR Fiqh schools (Hanafi, Maliki, Shafi'e, Hanbali) is that Tawarruq is permissible, though Qard al Hasan (interest-free lending) remains more preferable where available.","AAOIFI's ruling: selling the commodity back to the original deferred-payment seller (directly or indirectly, even through an agent) is invalid; selling to a genuine third party is acceptable.","A critical structuring pitfall: if a bank appoints the Mutawarriq as its agent to BOTH purchase AND sell the commodity to himself, this is invalid, since the two transactions become interdependent and the bank never genuinely takes possession or business risk.","Tawarruq conducted through commodity exchanges (where goods never actually transfer ownership, only brokers perform nominal agency services) is vulnerable to violating multiple valid-sale conditions.","Prominent scholars strongly caution against widespread Tawarruq use: Monzer Kahf states it must be 'completely out' of Islamic banking for individual consumers, while Nejatullah Siddiqi criticizes it as reducing to 'signing a number of papers' to get cash and collateral - the author warns wide, careless use creates systemic risk for the industry."],"definitions":[{"term":"Tawarruq","definition":"A technique of buying a commodity on credit and immediately reselling it (to a third party) for spot cash, purely to generate liquidity - the commodity transaction itself is not the buyer's actual need."},{"term":"Mutawarriq","definition":"The person who acquires liquidity through a Tawarruq arrangement by purchasing a commodity on credit and reselling it for cash."}],"conditions":["In Tawarruq, the commodity must be sold to a genuine third party, not back (directly or indirectly through an agent) to the original seller from whom it was purchased on credit.","If a bank appoints the Mutawarriq as its agent to both purchase and sell a commodity to himself, the transaction is invalid.","If the Mutawarriq's agency to sell a commodity purchased from the bank is stipulated as a condition within the sale contract itself, the transaction is invalid; if arranged after an unconditional sale, it is valid (though not advisable)."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Nejatullah Siddiqi's Critique of Tawarruq","kind":"textbook","text":"\"The client approaches the Islamic bank with a wish to have cash and a collateral and comes back with the desired cash after signing a number of papers\" - Siddiqi's critique, cited by the author as a warning about Tawarruq's potential to become a bare paperwork exercise disguising an interest loan."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes assume Tawarruq and Bai' al 'Inah are the same thing since both involve a double transaction to generate cash. The key distinction: Tawarruq resells to a genuine THIRD PARTY (acceptable); 'Inah resells back to the SAME original seller (prohibited)."],"distinctions":["Tawarruq (resale to a genuine third party, permissible per the preferred view of all four schools) vs Bai' al 'Inah (resale back to the original seller, prohibited)."],"relatedConcepts":["Bai' al 'Inah","Murabaha","Qard al Hasan"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349,350]},"transactionSteps":[{"step":"Customer Needs Cash","description":"The customer (Mutawarriq) does not actually want any commodity; he needs liquidity, so he approaches the bank for a Tawarruq-based cash financing arrangement."},{"step":"Bank Sells a Commodity on Deferred Payment","description":"The bank purchases a liquid, genuinely saleable commodity (e.g. metals, shares, Sukuk) and sells it to the customer on a Murabaha-style deferred-payment basis, at cost plus an agreed profit margin, with ownership and risk passing to the customer."},{"step":"Customer Takes Ownership","description":"Ownership and risk of the commodity transfer to the customer at the point of sale, satisfying the requirement that a genuine sale - not a disguised loan - has taken place."},{"step":"Commodity Resold Via an Agent (or the Customer Himself)","description":"The customer, either personally or via an agent appointed after the sale (not as a pre-arranged condition of it), arranges to resell the same commodity."},{"step":"Sale to a Genuine Third Party","description":"The commodity is sold in the open market to an independent third party unconnected to the original transaction, for cash at the prevailing spot price - this is the step that makes the transaction Shari'ah-compliant Tawarruq rather than prohibited Bai' al 'Inah, since the resale is NOT back to the original bank-seller."},{"step":"Customer Receives Cash","description":"The customer receives the spot-sale proceeds immediately, satisfying his original need for liquidity."},{"step":"Customer Owes the Bank the Deferred Price","description":"The customer now owes the bank the full deferred Murabaha price on the agreed schedule, independent of what the commodity actually resold for in the market."},{"step":"Repayment Over Time","description":"The customer repays the bank in installments (or as a lump sum) per the agreed schedule, completing the Tawarruq financing structure."}],"calculations":[{"title":"Practice Example — generated for learning: Tawarruq Cash Financing and Its Implied Cost","formula":"Implied financing cost (% p.a.) = [(Deferred Price - Cash Received) / Cash Received] x (12 / financing tenor in months) x 100","inputs":[{"label":"Cash the customer needs today","value":"$90,000"},{"label":"Bank's Murabaha sale price to customer (cost + profit), deferred","value":"$100,000"},{"label":"Financing tenor","value":"12 months"},{"label":"Third-party spot resale value of the same commodity","value":"approx. $90,000"}],"steps":["The bank buys a commodity for approximately $90,000 cash and sells it to the customer on Murabaha at a deferred price of $100,000, payable in 12 months.","The customer (or his agent, appointed after the sale) immediately resells the same commodity to an independent third party in the market for its cash value, approximately $90,000 - a genuine sale to a party other than the bank.","The customer nets $90,000 cash today from the third-party resale, meeting his original liquidity need.","The customer's total obligation to the bank is $100,000, payable over the 12-month tenor.","Implied financing markup = $100,000 - $90,000 = $10,000 over 12 months.","Implied annualized financing cost = ($10,000 / $90,000) x 100 = approximately 11.1% per annum (over the 12-month tenor)."],"result":"The customer receives $90,000 cash today and repays $100,000 over 12 months - an implied annualized financing cost of approximately 11.1%.","interpretation":"This is exactly why critics like Nejatullah Siddiqi describe Tawarruq as reducing to 'signing a number of papers' to get cash: economically, the customer ends up paying a markup that functions much like interest on a cash loan. What keeps it Shari'ah-compliant (per the preferred view across all four Fiqh schools), rather than a disguised Riba loan, is that two genuine sale contracts occur - the bank's sale of a real, owned commodity to the customer, and the customer's (or his agent's) sale of that same commodity to an unconnected THIRD PARTY - so the customer's obligation arises from real ownership and sale, not a cash loan repayable with interest. If the customer instead resold the commodity back to the original bank, the same $90,000-for-$100,000 economics would instead constitute prohibited Bai' al 'Inah, which is why the genuine third party in Step 5 is the structure's Shari'ah-critical element.","generated":true,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349,350]}}]}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Tawarruq","b":"‘Inah","text":"In Tawarruq the commodity is sold to a third party; in ‘Inah it returns to the original seller."},{"a":"Ibn Taymiyah and Ibn Qayyim","b":"Majority of Hanbalis","text":"Two versions are reported from Imam Ahmad. Most Hanbalis prefer the one permitting Tawarruq; Ibn Taymiyah and Ibn Qayyim held it impermissible (p. 349)."},{"a":"Some later Hanafis","b":"Ibnul-Hummam (majority Hanafi view)","text":"Some later Hanafis equated Tawarruq with ‘Inah and held it abominable. Ibnul-Hummam limited ‘Inah to resale to the original seller, so a sale in the market is valid, though Qard Hasan is preferable (p. 350)."}],"table":{"caption":"Organised Tawarruq structures (p. 350)","head":["Structure","Ruling"],"rows":[["Bank buys a commodity, sells to Mutawarriq, who sells it in the market","No Shari’ah problem"],["Mutawarriq is the bank’s agent to buy and then sells to himself","Invalid"],["Mutawarriq is agent only to buy; the bank then sells by a separate contract","Valid but not advisable"],["Bank is the Mutawarriq’s agent to sell, stipulated in the sale","Invalid"],["Bank is agent to sell, appointed after an unconditional sale","Valid but not advisable"],["Via commodity exchange where goods never move","Vulnerable to violations"]]},"debate":[{"issue":"Should Islamic banks use Tawarruq widely?","criticism":"Organised Tawarruq can become a paper exchange delivering cash for a fixed return — Siddiqi's ‘client wants cash and leaves with cash after signing papers’; Kahf says it should be wholly excluded for consumers.","response":"The preferred view in the four schools and AAOIFI permits genuine Tawarruq with sale to a third party and proper sale conditions.","alternative":"Use it only for unavoidable corporate liquidity needs, with strict Shari’ah board monitoring; prefer Qard al Hasan.","takeaway":"Legal permissibility does not justify extensive use; structure and scale both matter."},{"issue":"Should Tawarruq be used for consumer finance?","criticism":"Monzer Kahf says it should be completely out of Islamic banking for individual consumers. Nejatullah Siddiqi describes clients arriving wanting cash and leaving with cash after signing papers.","response":"Most schools and the AAOIFI accept Tawarruq in principle when the commodity is sold to a third party.","takeaway":"Scholars advise limiting it to unavoidable corporate liquidity needs (p. 350)."}]},{"id":"t13.3.1","section":"13.3.1","title":"Use of Tawarruq for Liquidity Management","pages":[351,351],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["tawarruq","liquidity"],"intuition":"How can two Islamic banks lend to each other overnight without interest?","simpleExplanation":"Used as Commodity or Shares Murabaha (widely in the Middle East) for a fixed return. Acceptable steps: both banks choose a liquid commodity or blue-chip shares; the surplus bank buys it for cash; the deficit bank buys from the surplus bank on Murabaha credit; after delivery it sells at spot in the market. It must not become a mere exchange of papers, should be used only where no other option avoids interest, and must be strictly monitored by Shari’ah boards.","academicExplanation":["Widespread use is harmful to the industry in the long run.","Commodity Murabaha or Shares Murabaha, widely used in the Middle East, lets banks place and raise funds at a fixed return. In an acceptable structure the two banks choose a liquid commodity or blue-chip stock. The surplus bank buys it for cash in the market. The deficit bank buys it from the surplus bank on credit (Murabaha), takes delivery and sells it at spot. The risk is that this becomes a mere exchange of papers between brokers and banks. The book therefore treats it as a last resort where there is no other way to avoid interest, and asks Shari’ah boards to monitor such deals closely (p. 351)."],"examExplanation":"Interbank Tawarruq: liquid asset chosen → surplus bank buys cash → deficit bank buys on Murabaha credit → takes delivery and sells spot. Safeguards: real transfer, last resort, Shari’ah board monitoring.","keyPoints":["Known as Commodity Murabaha / Shares Murabaha.","The deficit bank must take delivery before selling at spot (p. 351)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Interbank Commodity Murabaha","kind":"practice","text":"Bank D needs 50 million for 3 months. Bank S buys 50 million of blue-chip shares in the market, sells them to D on 3-month credit for 50.6 million, and D sells them at spot. D has cash now and owes 50.6 million at maturity (p. 351)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Interbank Tawarruq is harmless because it is simple.","right":"The book warns that widespread use harms the industry in the long run and must be strictly monitored by Shari’ah boards (p. 351)."}],"relatedTopics":["t14.4.4","t13.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In interbank Tawarruq, who buys the commodity on deferred payment?","options":["The surplus bank","The deficit bank","The broker","The central bank"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 351."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch13-t5"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch13-t5","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"13.3.1","sectionTitle":"Use of Tawarruq for Liquidity Management","title":"Genuine Tawarruq for Inter-bank Liquidity Management","covers":["t13.3.1"],"overview":"Describes how banks use Tawarruq (commodity or shares Murabaha) to place and obtain funds between each other, and the author's strong caution against overuse.","simpleExplanation":"Some Islamic banks use Tawarruq to move money between each other when one has a surplus and another needs funds - it's popular in the Middle East as 'Commodity Murabaha' or 'Shares Murabaha' because it can deliver a fixed-like return. The basic setup: the surplus bank buys a liquid commodity or stock with cash, then sells it to the deficit bank on credit (Murabaha); once the deficit bank takes delivery, it immediately resells it in the market for cash. This sounds simple, but banks need to be extremely careful it doesn't become a meaningless shuffle of paperwork between brokers - the author insists it should only be used as a last resort, not routinely, because widespread use risks real damage to the industry's credibility.","academicExplanation":"Some Islamic banks use Tawarruq specifically to PLACE and OBTAIN funds between institutions - it can deliver something close to a fixed return, making it widely used as Commodity Murabaha or Shares Murabaha in the Middle East. Acceptable Tawarruq arrangement structure: one bank (needing funds) and another (with funds to place) select a liquid commodity/stock (e.g. blue-chip stocks); the SURPLUS bank purchases the commodity for cash from the market; the DEFICIT bank then purchases it from the surplus bank ON CREDIT (structured as Murabaha), and after taking delivery, immediately sells it in the market at spot price. The process appears simple, but EXTREME CARE is required to ensure it doesn't degenerate into a mere paper exchange between brokers with no genuine underlying transaction. Islamic banks must understand Tawarruq should be used in EXTREME CASES ONLY where no alternative exists to avoid interest - widespread use of such products is harmful to the Islamic banking industry in the long run, and Shari'ah boards need to STRICTLY MONITOR all Tawarruq-based transactions.","examExplanation":"Inter-bank liquidity Tawarruq structure: surplus bank buys a liquid commodity/stock for CASH -> sells it to the deficit bank ON CREDIT (Murabaha structure) -> deficit bank takes delivery, immediately resells it at SPOT price in the market for cash. Widely used as 'Commodity Murabaha' or 'Shares Murabaha' in the Middle East, since it can deliver a near-fixed return. Critical caution: must not degenerate into a mere paper/brokerage exchange with no genuine transaction; should be used ONLY in extreme cases with no alternative to avoid interest; Shari'ah boards must strictly monitor all such transactions given the risk to the industry's long-term credibility.","keyPoints":["Genuine inter-bank Tawarruq structure: the surplus bank buys a liquid commodity/stock for cash, sells it to the deficit bank on credit (structured as Murabaha), and the deficit bank immediately resells it at spot price in the market for cash - widely used as 'Commodity Murabaha' or 'Shares Murabaha,' especially in the Middle East.","Extreme care is required to ensure the arrangement involves a genuine underlying transaction, not a mere paper/brokerage exchange between two banks.","The author's firm position: Tawarruq should be used only in extreme cases where no alternative exists to avoid interest - widespread, routine use is harmful to the Islamic banking industry's long-term credibility.","Shari'ah boards need to strictly monitor all Tawarruq-based transactions given these structuring risks."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["Inter-bank Tawarruq arrangements must involve a genuine underlying commodity/stock transaction, not merely a paper exchange between brokers."],"principles":["Tawarruq should be used only in extreme cases where no alternative exists to avoid interest; its widespread, routine use is harmful to the Islamic banking industry in the long run."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Inter-bank Commodity Murabaha (Tawarruq)","kind":"textbook","text":"A surplus bank purchases a liquid commodity or blue-chip stock for cash. A deficit bank purchases it from the surplus bank on credit (Murabaha structure) and, after taking delivery, immediately sells it in the market at spot price to generate the cash liquidity it needs."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think genuine inter-bank Tawarruq (Commodity/Shares Murabaha) is a routine, low-risk liquidity tool simply because it is widely used. The author explicitly warns it should remain an extreme-case last resort, given the risk of it degenerating into a mere paper exchange and the systemic credibility risk of widespread use."],"distinctions":["Genuine Tawarruq (real commodity/stock changes ownership between distinct parties) vs a degenerated paper exchange (goods never genuinely transfer, only brokers perform nominal agency services) - the latter is vulnerable to Shari'ah violations."],"relatedConcepts":["Commodity Murabaha","Inter-bank Funds Market","Bai' al Sarf"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3.1","pages":[351]}}],"processSteps":["Banks select a liquid commodity or blue-chip stocks","Surplus bank buys it for cash in the market","Deficit bank buys it from the surplus bank on deferred Murabaha","Deficit bank takes delivery and sells at spot for cash","Deficit bank pays the Murabaha price at maturity"]},{"id":"t13.4","section":"13.4","title":"Ju‘alah","pages":[351,351],"tier":"core","concepts":["jualah"],"intuition":"How do you contract for a result when you cannot specify the work?","simpleExplanation":"Ju‘alah is a contract in which one party (Ja‘il) promises a specific reward (Ju‘l) to whoever achieves a specified result — e.g. finding a stolen car. It is permissible by Qur’an (the reward for the King's lost beaker in Surah Yusuf) and Sunnah (Companions' reward for curing a tribal chief). It suits tasks Ijarah cannot handle because Ijarah requires the work to be specified — such as recovering overdue debts.","academicExplanation":["Some jurists restrict Ju‘alah to the return of a runaway slave, but the majority allow many activities; determination of the required end result suffices."],"examExplanation":"Ju‘alah: reward for achieving a specified result; Qur’an (Yusuf) and Sunnah; used where work cannot be specified (debt recovery).","keyPoints":["Result specified, work may be uncertain."],"definitions":[{"term":"Ju‘alah","definition":"A contract in which the offeror (Ja‘il) promises a known reward (Ju‘l) to whoever achieves a specified result."},{"term":"Ja‘il","definition":"The offeror in Ju‘alah, who promises the reward."},{"term":"Ju‘l","definition":"The reward payable on achieving the result in Ju‘alah."}],"examples":[{"title":"The King’s beaker (Surah Yusuf)","kind":"textbook","text":"A camel-load of grain was announced for whoever found the King’s lost beaker, the Qur’anic basis cited for Ju‘alah (p. 351)."},{"title":"Curing the tribal chief","kind":"textbook","text":"The Prophet (pbuh) approved some Companions’ condition that they would be compensated if the chief of a tribe was cured (p. 351)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Ju‘alah is limited to rewards for returning a runaway slave.","right":"Some jurists restricted it so, but the majority allow it for many activities, provided the required end result is determined (p. 351)."}],"relatedTopics":["t13.4.1","t11.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why is Ju‘alah used where Ijarah is unsuitable?","options":["Ju‘alah is cheaper","Ijarah requires the work to be specified; Ju‘alah needs only the result","Ju‘alah has no reward","Ijarah is prohibited"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 351."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch13-t6"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch13-t6","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"13.4","sectionTitle":"Ju'alah","title":"Ju'alah: Concept, Permissibility, and Distinction from Ijarah","covers":["t13.4","t13.4.1","t13.4.2","t13.4.3","t13.4.4","t13.4.5","t13.4.6"],"overview":"Defines Ju'alah (a reward-for-result contract), its Qur'anic and Prophetic basis, and why it uniquely suits tasks whose work or outcome cannot be precisely specified in advance.","simpleExplanation":"Ju'alah is a 'reward for results' contract - one party (the Ja'il) promises a specific reward to WHOEVER manages to achieve a certain outcome, like finding a lost item. It's backed directly by the Qur'an (the story of the King's lost drinking cup, where a reward of a camel-load of grain was offered) and by the Prophet's (pbuh) own approval of a similar deal. What makes Ju'alah special is that it works for tasks that Ijarah (regular hiring) CAN'T handle - because Ijarah requires the work to be precisely specified upfront, but Ju'alah doesn't care exactly how much work it takes, only whether the result actually gets achieved.","academicExplanation":"Ju'alah is a contract where one party (the Ja'il) undertakes to give a specific reward (the Jua'l) to ANYONE who is able to realize a specific or uncertain required result (e.g. finding a stolen car). Ju'alah is permissible on the authority of the Qur'an and Sunnah: Surah Yousuf references the King's lost beaker, with an announcement that whoever finds it would receive a camel-load of grain as reward; as for Sunnah, the Prophet (pbuh) approved a deal by some Companions who stipulated compensation if they could cure a tribal chief. Although some jurists restrict Ju'alah narrowly to rewards for returning a runaway slave, the MAJORITY consider it permissible for a broader range of activities - the determination of the required END RESULT is considered sufficient to make the contract permissible. Ju'alah is especially relevant/useful for tasks Ijarah CANNOT accomplish - such as recovering lost property from an uncertain location - because Ijarah's structure REQUIRES the work itself to be precisely specified, whereas Ju'alah does not. Accordingly, Islamic banks can use Ju'alah for recovering overdue debts and other services where the required work cannot be minutely specified in advance.","examExplanation":"Ju'alah = 'reward for result' contract; Ja'il (offeror) promises a reward (Jua'l) to WHOEVER achieves a specific/uncertain result. Basis: Qur'an (Surah Yousuf, King's lost beaker - camel-load of grain reward) and Sunnah (Prophet's approval of a curing-the-chief reward deal). Majority view: broadly permissible (not restricted to runaway-slave recovery). KEY DISTINCTION FROM IJARAH: Ijarah requires the WORK ITSELF to be precisely specified upfront; Ju'alah only requires the END RESULT to be determined - making it uniquely suited to tasks with unspecifiable/uncertain work (e.g. recovering lost property, overdue debt collection).","keyPoints":["Ju'alah is a contract where one party (Ja'il) promises a specific reward (Jua'l) to whoever manages to achieve a specific or uncertain required result.","Ju'alah's permissibility rests on Qur'anic authority (Surah Yousuf's account of the King's lost beaker, with a camel-load-of-grain reward offered) and Sunnah (the Prophet's approval of a Companions' curing-the-chief reward deal).","The majority of jurists consider Ju'alah permissible for a broad range of activities (not just the narrow classical example of runaway-slave recovery); determination of the required END RESULT is sufficient to make the contract valid.","The key distinction from Ijarah: Ijarah requires the WORK ITSELF to be precisely specified upfront (making it unsuitable for uncertain-outcome tasks); Ju'alah only requires the END RESULT to be determined, making it uniquely suited to tasks like recovering lost property from an uncertain location or collecting overdue debts."],"definitions":[{"term":"Ju'alah","definition":"A contract where one party (the Ja'il) undertakes to give a specific reward (the Jua'l) to anyone able to realize a specific or uncertain required result."},{"term":"Ja'il","definition":"The offeror in a Ju'alah contract, who promises the reward for achievement of the specified result."},{"term":"Jua'l","definition":"The reward promised by the Ja'il in a Ju'alah contract, payable only upon actual realization of the required result."}],"conditions":[],"principles":["Ju'alah's validity requires only that the required END RESULT of the transaction be determined - unlike Ijarah, which requires the underlying work itself to be precisely specified."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"The King's Lost Beaker (Surah Yousuf)","kind":"textbook","text":"Surah Yousuf references an announcement about the King's lost drinking beaker: whoever found and returned it would receive a reward of a camel-load of grain - cited as Qur'anic textual authority for the permissibility of Ju'alah."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think Ju'alah and Ijarah are interchangeable ways to pay for a service. They are not: Ijarah requires precisely specified work (payment owed regardless of outcome, if the agreed time/work is completed); Ju'alah requires only a specified result (payment owed only if the result is actually achieved, regardless of how much work it took)."],"distinctions":["Ijarah (work must be precisely specified upfront, payment owed for time/work regardless of outcome) vs Ju'alah (only the end result must be specified, payment owed only if the result is actually achieved)."],"relatedConcepts":["Ijarah","Wakalah","Ujrah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4","pages":[351]},"transactionSteps":[{"step":"Ja'il Announces Task and Reward","description":"The Ja'il (offeror) publicly announces, or privately agrees, a specific or uncertain task (e.g. finding a lost item, curing an illness, recovering a debt) along with a known, valuable, deliverable reward (the Jua'l), which may be a lump sum or a percentage of the realized result."},{"step":"Worker Undertakes the Task","description":"Any interested party (if the offer is open to the general public) or a specified named worker (if the Ju'alah is concluded with a specific person) undertakes the task; no formal counter-acceptance is required, since attempting the task itself constitutes engagement with the offer."},{"step":"Work Is Attempted","description":"The worker applies effort toward achieving the specified result; the exact scope or amount of work needed can remain uncertain without invalidating the contract, since only the end result - not the work itself - must be determined."},{"step":"Contract Becomes Binding Once Work Begins","description":"Once the worker commences work, the Ja'il can no longer freely cancel without owing a reasonable wage for work already done (though the worker himself may still withdraw with no claim against the offeror, unless otherwise agreed)."},{"step":"Task Completed (or Not)","description":"The worker either succeeds in realizing the specified result (e.g. the lost item is found, the debt is collected) or fails despite genuine effort."},{"step":"Reward Paid Only If Realized","description":"If the result is realized, the Ja'il pays the agreed Jua'l; if the worker's genuine effort fails to achieve the result, no reward is owed for the effort or time spent - subject to narrow exceptions, such as a non-negligent accident impairing the subject matter, which entitles the worker to the full reward."}]}]},{"id":"t13.4.1","section":"13.4.1","title":"Parties to Ju‘alah","pages":[351,352],"tier":"core","concepts":["jualah"],"intuition":"If the worker tries hard but fails, is anything owed?","simpleExplanation":"The offeror promises compensation to the worker — a specified person or the public — for a result in a known or unknown period. No result, no reward. Ju‘alah is non-binding and either party may terminate, but once work starts, an offeror who revokes must pay reasonable wages; a worker who revokes after starting has no claim unless agreed. The worker is a trustee of the offeror's property.","academicExplanation":["If a specific worker is named, he must work himself or involve others with the offeror's express consent. Ju‘alah resembles agency in allowing help from others.","Where parties undertake not to terminate within a period, the undertaking must be observed.","Ju‘alah has two parties: the offeror, and the worker, who may be a named person or anyone from the public. Payment depends entirely on achieving the result. A worker who tries and fails gets nothing, and this is why uncertainty about the work does not spoil the contract (pp. 351–352). A named worker must do the work himself unless the offeror expressly agrees to others helping. A worker who responds to a public offer may use helpers. The worker holds the offeror’s property as a trustee and is liable only for negligence, misconduct or breach of conditions (p. 352)."],"examExplanation":"Parties: offeror and worker (specific or public). No result → no reward. Non-binding; revocation by offeror after commencement → reasonable wage. Worker = trustee.","keyPoints":["Different from Ijarah: the worker may be unspecified.","Ju‘alah is not binding in itself, but an undertaking not to terminate for a set period must be honoured (p. 352)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Public offer of a reward","kind":"textbook","text":"A company announces a reward of 1 million to anyone who finds its lost container. Anyone may act, alone or with helpers. Whoever finds it earns the reward; those who searched without success get nothing (pp. 351–352)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"If the offeror revokes after work has started, the worker gets nothing because the result was not achieved.","right":"Once work has begun, revocation by the offeror obliges him to pay a reasonable wage for the work done (p. 352)."},{"wrong":"A worker who abandons the job midway can claim for his effort.","right":"If the worker revokes after starting, he has no claim unless agreed otherwise (p. 352)."}],"relatedTopics":["t13.4.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"The offeror revokes a Ju‘alah after the worker has started. The worker is entitled to:","options":["Nothing","A reasonable wage","The full reward","Double the reward"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 352."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch13-t7"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch13-t7","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"13.4.1","sectionTitle":"Parties to Ju'alah","title":"Parties, Binding Nature, and Termination of Ju'alah","covers":["t13.4.1"],"overview":"Explains the offeror-worker relationship in Ju'alah, why payment depends strictly on realizing the result, and when the otherwise-cancellable contract becomes binding once work starts.","simpleExplanation":"Ju'alah has an offeror (who promises the reward) and a worker (who tries to earn it) - the worker can be a specific named person or just 'anyone from the public' who wants to take a shot at it. Crucially, if the worker tries hard but fails to achieve the result, they get NOTHING - payment only happens if the actual goal is met. Either side can normally cancel the deal freely, but once the worker actually starts working, the offeror can't just cancel for free anymore - they'd owe the worker a reasonable wage for effort spent (loss shouldn't fall on the worker for legitimate work already done). The worker is treated like a trustee for any property in his care and is only liable if he's negligent or misbehaves.","academicExplanation":"The two parties to Ju'alah are the OFFEROR (Ja'il) and the WORKER - the offeror offers specified compensation to anyone (the worker) who realizes a determined result within a known or unknown period. The worker may be any specified person(s) OR the general public - this differs from Ijarah. REALIZATION of the end result is NECESSARY for the worker's compensation entitlement - if the worker, despite genuine effort, fails to realize the objective, he gets NO compensation for his effort/time. This is precisely why Ju'alah is unaffected by uncertainty regarding the subject matter/work, making it suitable for activities Ijarah cannot handle. It is NOT a condition that the worker be specified - an offer to the general public suffices, in which case any interested person may undertake the work himself or with others' help. If a SPECIFIC worker is named, however, he must undertake the work HIMSELF, or involve others only with the offeror's EXPRESS consent - Ju'alah is similar to agency in this respect, where seeking others' help is valid. Ju'alah is NOT a binding contract per se - either party can terminate unilaterally. HOWEVER, once the worker COMMENCES work, it becomes binding, and if the offeror revokes in between, he must give the worker a REASONABLE WAGE - the entitlement basis being that the work done is legally valid and the worker should not suffer loss for it. If the parties agreed not to terminate within a specified period, they must observe that undertaking. If the WORKER HIMSELF revokes after commencing work, he has NO claim against the offeror, unless otherwise agreed. The worker is considered a TRUSTEE regarding any offeror property in his possession - not liable for loss except in cases of negligence, misconduct, or violation of stipulated conditions.","examExplanation":"Ju'alah parties: Ja'il (offeror) + worker (specified person(s) OR the general public - unlike Ijarah). Payment STRICTLY conditional on ACTUALLY realizing the result - failed effort earns nothing. If worker is UNSPECIFIED (open public offer), anyone can attempt it (alone or with help); if SPECIFIED, must personally undertake it (or involve others only with offeror's express consent). Binding nature: NOT binding before work starts (either party can terminate freely); BECOMES binding once worker COMMENCES work - offeror revoking then must pay a REASONABLE WAGE (not the full reward, since result wasn't achieved, but compensation for legitimate work done). Worker revoking after starting = no claim against offeror (unless otherwise agreed). Worker = trustee for offeror's property; liable only for negligence/misconduct/condition violation.","keyPoints":["Ju'alah's worker can be any specified person(s) or simply the general public (unlike Ijarah) - if specified, he must personally undertake the work (or involve others only with the offeror's express consent); if unspecified, any interested party may attempt it, alone or with help.","Payment is strictly conditional on actually realizing the required result - a worker who genuinely attempts but fails to achieve the objective receives no compensation for his time or effort.","Ju'alah is not a binding contract per se; either party can terminate unilaterally before work commences.","Once the worker commences work, the contract becomes binding - if the offeror revokes at that point, he must pay the worker a reasonable wage (not the full reward), since the worker's legitimate effort should not go uncompensated.","If the worker himself revokes the contract after commencing work, he has no claim against the offeror, unless otherwise agreed.","The worker is considered a trustee regarding any offeror property in his possession, liable only for loss caused by negligence, misconduct, or violation of stipulated conditions."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["Payment to a Ju'alah worker is due only upon actual realization of the required result, not merely for effort expended.","A specified worker in Ju'alah must personally undertake the work, or involve others only with the offeror's express consent.","Once the worker commences work, the offeror cannot revoke the contract without paying a reasonable wage."],"principles":["The worker's entitlement to reasonable wages upon the offeror's mid-work revocation rests on the principle that legally valid work already performed should not cause the worker loss."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students often think a Ju'alah worker who tries hard but fails still deserves some payment. The rule is strict: realization of the actual result is necessary for compensation - genuine effort alone, without success, earns nothing, unless the offeror separately revokes the contract mid-work (in which case a reasonable wage, not the full reward, is owed)."],"distinctions":["A Ju'alah worker's failed genuine effort (no compensation at all) vs the offeror's mid-work unilateral revocation (worker owed a reasonable wage for work already done)."],"relatedConcepts":["Wakalah","Ijarah","Amanah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.1","pages":[351,352]}}]},{"id":"t13.4.2","section":"13.4.2","title":"Subject Matter of Ju‘alah and Reward","pages":[352,353],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["jualah"],"intuition":"Can a reward be a percentage of what is recovered?","simpleExplanation":"The subject matter is the work and the agreed compensation. The task must involve effort and not be the worker's legal or employment duty; the reward must be known, valuable and deliverable — or a portion of the result. Uses: mineral extraction, finding lost property, debt collection (reward proportionate to recoveries), reports, inventions, trademark designs, brokerage (reward on contract execution). A time limit may be set and extended by consent.","academicExplanation":["The contract is valid despite uncertainty about the amount of work and the probability of success, provided the result is determined.","The subject matter of Ju‘alah is the task and the reward. The task must require some effort and must not already be a legal or employment duty of the worker. The reward must be known, valuable and deliverable. It may be a share of the result, such as a percentage of debts recovered (p. 352). Once the result is defined, the contract is valid even though the amount of work and the chance of success are unknown. Uses include mineral extraction, finding lost property, debt collection, reports, scientific inventions, designs such as trademarks, and brokerage, where the reward falls due when the brokered contract is concluded. The offeror may set a deadline, and the parties may extend it by consent if the work is well advanced but delayed for a genuine reason (pp. 352–353)."],"examExplanation":"Task: effortful, not a legal/employment duty. Reward: known, lawful, deliverable, or share of result. Uses: minerals, lost property, debts, reports, inventions, brokerage.","keyPoints":["Reward can be a proportion of the realised result."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Mineral exploration","kind":"textbook","text":"A government offers a fixed sum only to firms that find an agreed mineral with specified features (p. 352)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A police officer may claim a Ju‘alah reward for recovering stolen property in the course of his duty.","right":"The task must not be a legal or employment obligation on the worker (p. 352)."}],"relatedTopics":["t13.4.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which cannot be the task of a Ju‘alah?","options":["Finding a lost car","Collecting defaulted debts","A duty the worker already owes by law or employment","Discovering a mineral"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"p. 352."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch13-t8"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch13-t8","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"13.4.2-13.4.3","sectionTitle":"Subject Matter of Ju'alah and Reward / Execution of a Ju'alah Contract","title":"Subject Matter, Reward Rules, and Execution of Ju'alah","covers":["t13.4.2","t13.4.3"],"overview":"Details what makes a valid Ju'alah task and reward, real-world application areas, and how the contract is formed and enforced without requiring counter-acceptance.","simpleExplanation":"The task in a Ju'alah must involve real effort and can't be something the worker was already legally or professionally obligated to do anyway. The reward has to be something known, valuable, and actually deliverable - it can even just be a percentage of whatever gets recovered (like a cut of collected debt). Ju'alah works well for things like mineral prospecting, finding lost items, debt collection, research reports, inventions, and brokerage. Unlike most contracts, you don't need the other side to formally 'accept' upfront - if it's an open public offer, anyone who steps up and does the job has effectively accepted just by doing it. Normally you only get paid once the job's actually done, though there are a couple of special exceptions - like if an accident (not your fault) ruins the thing you were working on, you still get the full reward.","academicExplanation":"The subject matter of Ju'alah is the required WORK and the AGREED COMPENSATION. As Ju'alah is a contract of exchange, both a task and a reward must be indicated. The task must NOT be a pre-existing legal or employment obligation on the worker, and must involve some EFFORT. The reward must be known and valuable (permissible consideration) and DELIVERABLE when required, to avoid uncertainty upon realization of the result - the reward CAN also be a certain PORTION of the realized result (e.g. a percentage of collected debt). If the required work-result is DETERMINED, a Ju'alah contract is valid DESPITE uncertainty about how much work the worker will put in and the possibility of actually realizing the result. Application areas: mineral extraction, finding lost assets/property, debt collection, information-gathering (reports on a subject/project), scientific invention, brokerage. Example: governments may commission firms for mineral extraction, paying specified compensation only to those who find agreed-upon minerals with specified features. For debt collection Ju'alah, compensation entitlement is contingent on COLLECTION of agreed debts/receivables, and can be proportionate to the amount actually realized. For innovations/discoveries/designs (e.g. trademarks), entitlement is contingent on ACTUAL realization of the discovery or stipulated job accomplishment. For BROKERAGE Ju'alah, entitlement is contingent on completion/execution of the brokered contract. An offeror CAN specify a completion time deadline, after which the worker loses entitlement - unless otherwise stipulated (e.g. parties can agree an advanced-but-incomplete job's deadline can be extended with mutual consent for a genuine problem). Execution: Ju'alah can be concluded via an OPEN/INFORMAL public offer - anyone hearing/receiving the offer and interested may do the job (himself or with others' assistance); if concluded with a SPECIFIED worker, that worker must personally perform. CRUCIALLY, conclusion of a Ju'alah contract does NOT require COUNTER-ACCEPTANCE by another party (unlike Ijarah), because counter-acceptance is practically UNATTAINABLE for an open public offer - except when concluded with a specific worker who is obliged to perform himself. Unlike Ijarah/Ujrah (where a worker who agreed to work a stipulated TIME is entitled to a wage upon completing that TIME, regardless of whether the WORK itself is finished), in Ju'alah the reward claim is NOT ENFORCEABLE until the required work is COMPLETED. However, the worker IS entitled to reward BEFORE completion in two situations: (1) when it's found the worker worked on property NOT belonging to the offeror, per a legal decision to that effect; (2) when an accident NOT due to the worker's negligence/misconduct impairs the subject matter's value - in this case, the worker is entitled to the FULL reward. The worker is NOT entitled to any reward if Ju'alah is terminated unilaterally by EITHER party BEFORE work commences. However, if the offeror terminates AFTER work commences, he must pay the worker the COMMON MARKET REMUNERATION.","examExplanation":"Ju'alah subject matter: a defined TASK (not a pre-existing obligation, must involve effort) + a known/valuable/deliverable REWARD (can be a % of realized result, e.g. collected debt). Contract is VALID despite uncertainty about work quantity/realization possibility, as long as the END RESULT itself is determined. Applications: mineral extraction, lost-property recovery, debt collection, research/reports, inventions, brokerage. Execution: NO counter-acceptance needed (unlike Ijarah) for an open public offer - performing the work IS the acceptance; a SPECIFIED worker must personally perform. Payment timing: NOT enforceable until work is COMPLETED (unlike Ujrah, where time-based wage is owed regardless of work completion) - EXCEPT 2 special cases entitling early payment: (1) worker discovered to have worked on property not belonging to the offeror (per legal decision); (2) a non-negligent accident impairs the subject matter (worker gets FULL reward). Termination: before work starts = worker gets NOTHING; offeror terminates AFTER work starts = worker gets COMMON MARKET wage (not the full reward).","keyPoints":["A valid Ju'alah task must not be a pre-existing legal or employment obligation on the worker, and must involve genuine effort; the reward must be known, valuable, deliverable, and can even be a percentage of the realized result (e.g. a cut of collected debt).","A Ju'alah contract remains valid despite uncertainty about how much work the worker will put in or whether the result will actually be realized - only the required END RESULT itself must be determined.","Conclusion of a Ju'alah contract does NOT require counter-acceptance (unlike Ijarah), since this would be practically unattainable for an open public offer - performing the work itself constitutes acceptance, except when a specific worker is named.","Unlike Ijarah/Ujrah (where a time-based wage is owed once the stipulated time is worked, regardless of whether the work itself is finished), a Ju'alah reward claim is not enforceable until the required work is actually completed.","Two exceptions entitle the worker to reward BEFORE completion: discovering the worker actually worked on property not belonging to the offeror (per legal decision), or a non-negligent accident impairing the subject matter's value (worker gets the FULL reward in this case).","If either party terminates before work commences, the worker gets nothing; if the offeror terminates AFTER work has commenced, he must pay the worker the common market remuneration (not the full contracted reward)."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["The Ju'alah task must not be a pre-existing legal or employment obligation on the worker, and must involve genuine effort.","The Ju'alah reward must be known, valuable (permissible consideration), and deliverable when required.","A Ju'alah reward claim is not enforceable until the required work is actually completed, except in two specified situations entitling early payment."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Mineral Extraction Ju'alah","kind":"textbook","text":"A government may commission firms for mineral extraction, with the condition that a specified sum of money will be given only to those who find an agreed-upon mineral with specified features - compensation entitlement is contingent on actual realization of the discovery."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes confuse Ju'alah's payment timing with Ujrah's. In Ujrah, a worker who agreed to work a stipulated time gets paid for that time regardless of whether the work is finished; in Ju'alah, the reward is not enforceable until the work is actually completed (with two narrow exceptions)."],"distinctions":["Ju'alah (reward enforceable only upon actual completion, with two narrow early-payment exceptions) vs Ujrah (wage enforceable upon completing the stipulated TIME, regardless of whether the work itself is finished)."],"relatedConcepts":["Ijarah","Ujrah","Wakalah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.2-13.4.3","pages":[352,353]}}]},{"id":"t13.4.3","section":"13.4.3","title":"Execution of a Ju‘alah Contract","pages":[353,353],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["jualah"],"intuition":"Why does a public reward notice not need anyone to ‘accept’ it?","simpleExplanation":"Ju‘alah may be an open public offer; no counter-acceptance is needed (unlike Ijarah) except with a specified worker. The reward is claimable only on completion (unlike Ujrah, which is earned for time worked), except (1) when the property turns out not to belong to the offeror by legal decision, and (2) when an accident not caused by the worker impairs the subject matter — then full reward. Termination before work → no reward; offeror termination after work began → market remuneration.","academicExplanation":["A specified worker must perform the work himself.","A Ju‘alah may be concluded by a public or informal offer, and no counter-acceptance is needed, since it is practically unattainable from an unknown public. The exception is a contract with a named worker, who must then do the work himself. Unlike Ijarah, where a worker hired for a set time earns his wage for that time whether or not the job is finished, the Ju‘alah reward is not claimable until the work is complete (p. 353). The book gives two exceptions. The first is where the property turns out not to belong to the offeror and a legal decision says so. The second is where an accident, not caused by the worker’s negligence, impairs the subject matter; here the worker gets the full reward (p. 353)."],"examExplanation":"Open offer; no acceptance needed; reward on completion (two exceptions); termination rules.","keyPoints":["Ujrah: time-based entitlement; Ju‘l: result-based.","Termination by either party before work starts: no reward. Termination by the offeror after work starts: market remuneration is payable (p. 353)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Accident before completion","kind":"textbook","text":"A worker recovering a stolen truck under Ju‘alah has nearly finished when a flood, not his fault, damages the truck. The book says he is entitled to the full reward in this situation (p. 353)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t13.4.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Does a public Ju‘alah offer require counter-acceptance?","options":["Yes, always","No — except with a specified worker","Only in writing","Only by the government"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 353."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"distinctions":[{"a":"Ju‘alah reward (Ju‘l)","b":"Ijarah wage (Ujrah)","text":"Ju‘l is due only on achieving the result; Ujrah is earned for the stipulated time worked even if the task is incomplete."}]},{"id":"t13.4.4","section":"13.4.4","title":"Parallel Ju‘alah Contracts","pages":[353,353],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["jualah","parallel-contracts"],"intuition":"Can a bank take on a job and subcontract it?","simpleExplanation":"A bank that takes a Ju‘alah job may get it done through an independent Parallel Ju‘alah with a third party, unless the first contract requires the bank to do it itself. The bank may also act as offeror, for its own needs or to fulfil a parallel obligation; the two contracts remain independent.","academicExplanation":["Independence of the two contracts is essential.","A bank may act as the worker in one Ju‘alah and hire another party under a separate, Parallel Ju‘alah, unless the first contract requires it to do the work personally. It may equally act as offeror, whether it needs the work for itself or to meet its own obligation under another Ju‘alah. The two contracts must remain independent (p. 353)."],"examExplanation":"Parallel Ju‘alah: two independent contracts; bank as worker subcontracts unless personal performance required.","keyPoints":["Like Parallel Salam/Istisna‘a: independence required."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Bank in the middle","kind":"practice","text":"A bank undertakes to recover a client’s overdue receivables for 5 % of collections, then engages a collection firm for 3.5 % under a separate Parallel Ju‘alah. Each contract is independent; the bank keeps the difference (p. 353)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A bank may always subcontract Ju‘alah work through a Parallel Ju‘alah.","right":"Only if the first Ju‘alah does not require the bank to perform the work itself (p. 353)."}],"relatedTopics":["t13.4.5","t10.11.7"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"When can a bank not use a Parallel Ju‘alah?","options":["Never restricted","When the first contract requires the bank to do the work itself","When the reward is money","When the customer is a corporate"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 353."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch13-t9"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch13-t9","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"13.4.4-13.4.6","sectionTitle":"Parallel Ju'alah Contracts / Practical Process in Ju'alah by Islamic Banks / Some Islamic Financial Products Based on Ju'alah","title":"Parallel Ju'alah, the Bank's Practical Process, and Ju'alah-Based Products","covers":["t13.4.4","t13.4.5","t13.4.6"],"overview":"Explains how banks use Parallel Ju'alah to subcontract work to genuine third parties, walks through the five-step bank practical process, and surveys real Ju'alah-based products: debt collection, financing-facilitation, and brokerage.","simpleExplanation":"A bank can take on a Ju'alah job from a customer, then turn around and hire someone else to actually do the work through a completely separate 'Parallel Ju'alah' contract - the two deals stay independent of each other. In practice: the customer negotiates a reward for some uncertain task, the bank agrees after weighing costs and benefits, the bank then finds an outside expert to actually do it (paying them from its own pocket), and once done, the bank collects its own reward from the original customer. Real products built on this include: debt collection (a cut of whatever gets recovered), helping arrange financing (paid for prep work like feasibility studies), and brokerage (paid when a deal gets signed).","academicExplanation":"A bank, after TAKING ON work via Ju'alah, can get it DONE by others on the basis of a PARALLEL Ju'alah - the two contracts remain INDEPENDENT of each other. The bank may play the role of WORKER (signing the first Ju'alah), carrying out the work itself OR through another parallel contract with a third party (provided the first contract doesn't require the bank to do the work personally). It's also possible for the bank to play the role of OFFEROR for performance - whether it needs the work for its own benefit or to fulfil an obligation under a Parallel Ju'alah contract - again, the two contracts must remain independent. Practical bank process (5 steps, Figure 13.1): (1) The customer negotiates with the bank for performance of uncertain work within a specified time for an agreed reward. (2) The bank agrees to perform the work AFTER conducting a cost-benefit analysis, and a Ju'alah contract is entered between bank and customer. (3) The bank finds a WORKER with relevant expertise to perform the uncertain work on its behalf, forming a PARALLEL Ju'alah contract with him. (4) The worker completes the work, and the bank pays him an agreed wage/reward. (5) The bank collects ITS OWN reward from the original customer under the initial Ju'alah contract. Banks similarly can take services FROM others on Ju'alah terms - recovery of NONPERFORMING DEBTS is one such example. Financial products based on Ju'alah: (1) DEBT COLLECTION - a Ju'alah contract between a bank and another party for recovering due debts, where the reward is a PERCENTAGE of the amount collected; the reward can also be paid in advance, in full or part, before work completion - but in this case, the worker is NOT absolutely entitled until the required result is realized, so the payment is made 'ON ACCOUNT.' (2) SECURING PERMISSIBLE FINANCING FACILITY - a worker performs a service (e.g. preparing a feasibility study) that will lead the bank to agree to provide financing to the offeror. (3) BROKERAGE - entitlement to reward is attached to the signing of a contract intermediated by the broker.","examExplanation":"Parallel Ju'alah: bank = worker in Contract 1 (with the customer, taking on the job) AND offeror in Contract 2 (a SEPARATE, independent Parallel Ju'alah with a third-party expert who actually does the work). 5-step bank process: (1) customer negotiates uncertain-work task + reward; (2) bank agrees after cost-benefit analysis, signs Ju'alah with customer; (3) bank finds an expert worker, signs Parallel Ju'alah with him; (4) worker completes work, bank pays him; (5) bank collects its own reward from the original customer. Ju'alah-based products: DEBT COLLECTION (reward = % of amount collected; advance payment possible but treated as 'on account' until result is realized), FINANCING-FACILITATION (paid for feasibility-study-type prep work that secures bank financing), BROKERAGE (reward tied to the intermediated contract's signing).","keyPoints":["A bank can take on a Ju'alah job as 'worker' (from a customer) and then subcontract the actual work via a SEPARATE, independent Parallel Ju'alah contract with a genuine third-party expert.","The 5-step bank process: customer negotiates the task/reward -> bank agrees after cost-benefit analysis, signs the Ju'alah -> bank finds an expert worker via a Parallel Ju'alah -> the worker completes the work and is paid by the bank -> the bank collects its own reward from the original customer.","Banks can also take services FROM others on Ju'alah terms - recovery of nonperforming debts is a prime example.","In debt-collection Ju'alah, the reward is typically a percentage of the amount actually collected; advance payment is possible but is treated as 'on account,' since the worker is not absolutely entitled until the required result (collection) is realized.","Other real Ju'alah-based products include securing permissible financing facilities (paid for prep work like feasibility studies) and brokerage (reward tied to the signing of the intermediated contract)."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["In Parallel Ju'alah, the two contracts (the original with the customer, and the parallel one with the actual worker) must remain independent of each other."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Debt Collection via Ju'alah","kind":"textbook","text":"A Ju'alah contract for debt recovery is entered into between 'A Bank' and 'B Ltd', providing for the reward as a percentage of the amount collected. The reward can also be paid in advance, in full or in part, before completion of the work - but in such a case, the worker is not absolutely entitled to the reward until the required result is realized, so the payment is made 'on account.'"}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think the bank's Parallel Ju'alah contract with the actual worker is legally tied to the original contract with the customer. The chapter is explicit that the two contracts remain fully independent of each other."],"distinctions":["The bank's role as WORKER (in the first Ju'alah, taking on the customer's job) vs its role as OFFEROR (in the Parallel Ju'alah, hiring the actual expert to do the work)."],"relatedConcepts":["Wakalah","Non-Performing Debts","Brokerage"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.4-13.4.6","pages":[353,354,355]}}]},{"id":"t13.4.5","section":"13.4.5","title":"Practical Process in Ju‘alah by Islamic Banks","pages":[353,354],"tier":"core","concepts":["jualah"],"intuition":"Trace the five arrows of Figure 13.1.","simpleExplanation":"(1) Customer negotiates with the bank for uncertain work within a time for a reward; (2) bank, after cost–benefit analysis, signs a Ju‘alah contract; (3) bank finds an expert and signs a Parallel Ju‘alah; (4) worker completes the work and is paid; (5) bank collects its reward from the customer. Banks may also hire others on Ju‘alah, e.g. to recover non-performing debts.","academicExplanation":["Figure 13.1 depicts the customer–bank–worker flow.","The book sets out a five-step process (Figure 13.1). The customer asks the bank to perform uncertain work within a set time for an agreed reward. The bank weighs cost against benefit and signs a Ju‘alah. It then engages an expert worker under a Parallel Ju‘alah. The worker completes the job and is paid by the bank. Finally, the bank collects its reward from the customer. The same logic lets a bank buy services such as recovery of non-performing debts (pp. 353–354)."],"examExplanation":"Figure 13.1 five steps: negotiate → Ju‘alah → Parallel Ju‘alah → work done, worker paid → bank collects reward.","keyPoints":["Bank's margin = customer reward − worker reward."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Margin on a parallel contract","kind":"practice","text":"A customer promises the bank 500,000 for tracing and recovering a shipment within 3 months. The bank engages an agency under Parallel Ju‘alah for 400,000. If the shipment is recovered, the bank pays 400,000 and collects 500,000. If not, nothing is payable under either contract (based on pp. 353–354)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t13.4.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In Figure 13.1, when does the bank collect its reward?","options":["Before any work","After the work is completed","When signing the parallel contract","Never"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 354."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"processSteps":["Customer negotiates uncertain work, time and reward with bank","Bank signs Ju‘alah after cost–benefit analysis","Bank signs Parallel Ju‘alah with an expert worker","Worker completes the work and receives agreed reward","Bank collects its reward from the customer"]},{"id":"t13.4.6","section":"13.4.6","title":"Islamic Financial Products Based on Ju‘alah","pages":[354,355],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["jualah"],"intuition":"Where might your bank use Ju‘alah without calling it that?","simpleExplanation":"Debt collection: reward as a percentage of amounts collected; advance payment is allowed but only ‘on account’ until the result is achieved. Securing permissible financing: a worker prepares, e.g., a feasibility study that leads the bank to grant a facility. Brokerage: reward on signing of the intermediated contract.","academicExplanation":["Advance reward is not absolutely earned until the result is realised.","The book lists three product uses. The first is debt collection: the reward is a percentage of the amount collected. It may be paid in advance, wholly or partly, but only on account until the result is achieved. The second is securing permissible finance: a worker prepares, for example, a feasibility study that leads a bank to grant the offeror a facility. The third is brokerage: the reward falls due when the brokered contract is signed (pp. 354–355)."],"examExplanation":"Ju‘alah products: debt collection (% of recovery; advances on account), securing permissible financing (feasibility), brokerage.","keyPoints":["Advance = on account only."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Brokerage by Ju‘alah","kind":"textbook","text":"A broker is promised 1 % of the price if he finds a buyer for a factory. He earns it only when the sale contract he arranged is signed, however much effort he spent before (p. 355)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A debt-recovery agent paid in advance keeps the advance whatever happens.","right":"Advance reward is on account; the worker is not absolutely entitled until the result is achieved (p. 355)."}],"relatedTopics":["t13.4.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A debt-collection Ju‘alah pays part of the reward in advance. The worker's right to it is:","options":["Absolute immediately","On account until the required result is realised","Forfeited","Interest"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 355."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t13.5","section":"13.5","title":"Bai‘ al Istijrar (Supply Contract)","pages":[355,355],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["istijrar"],"intuition":"How does a bakery supplying bread daily and billing monthly fit Islamic sale rules?","simpleExplanation":"Istijrar is not a separate mode but a repeat-sale arrangement: a supplier sells units from time to time, or in consignments, with price fixed in advance, per consignment or after all deliveries, on normal cash or credit terms. Scholars relax price fixation and payment rules for day-to-day trade absent Gharar (e.g. bread supplied for a month, paid monthly). If based on Murabaha or Salam, their conditions apply — e.g. separate offer and acceptance for every Murabaha consignment.","academicExplanation":["It works as a master agreement for ongoing financing under suitable normal modes, typical between wholesalers and retailers.","Istijrar is not a mode in itself. It is a framework for repeated ordinary sales in which a supplier provides amounts of a commodity from time to time, or delivers one agreed quantity in several consignments. The price may be fixed in advance, with each consignment, or after all deliveries. It usually takes the form of a master agreement between wholesaler and retailer, and scholars allow some flexibility on fixing and paying the price where there is no Gharar (p. 355). If a specific mode is used, its conditions still apply in full. Under Murabaha, for example, each consignment needs its own offer and acceptance based on the client’s requisition (p. 355)."],"examExplanation":"Istijrar: repeat supply sale under a master agreement; price in advance/per consignment/after deliveries; relaxation if no Gharar; Murabaha-based needs separate offer/acceptance per consignment.","keyPoints":["Not a specific mode."],"definitions":[{"term":"Bai‘ al Istijrar","definition":"A repeat supply arrangement in which a seller delivers a commodity from time to time on agreed price and payment terms."}],"examples":[{"title":"Monthly bread supply","kind":"textbook","text":"A baker supplies bread to a retailer throughout the month at a known price and is paid once at the month’s end (p. 355)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"An Istijrar master agreement lets a bank skip separate Murabaha contracts for each delivery.","right":"Where Murabaha is the mode, each consignment still needs separate offer and acceptance (p. 355)."}],"relatedTopics":["t9.7"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"If an Istijrar arrangement is based on Murabaha, what is needed for each consignment?","options":["Nothing extra","Separate offer and acceptance","A new master agreement","Takaful"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 355."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch13-t10"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch13-t10","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"13.5","sectionTitle":"Bai' al Istijrar (Supply Contract)","title":"Bai' al Istijrar: The Repeat-Sale Supply Contract","covers":["t13.5"],"overview":"Defines Istijrar as an ongoing, repeat-purchase supply arrangement, explains the relaxed Gharar-related flexibility Shari'ah scholars allow for it as normal day-to-day business, and clarifies that any specific mode used within it (e.g. Murabaha) must still fulfil its own full conditions.","simpleExplanation":"Istijrar isn't really its own separate financing mode - it's just an ongoing 'keep supplying me this stuff regularly' arrangement, like a bakery delivering bread to a shop every day and only getting paid once a month. Because this is such normal, everyday business (think wholesaler-retailer relationships), scholars allow some flexibility on exactly how and when the price gets fixed and paid, as long as there's no real Gharar (excessive uncertainty) involved. But if the repeat-supply deal is actually structured using a specific mode like Murabaha, then every single delivery still needs its own proper offer and acceptance, based on the customer's request each time.","academicExplanation":"Istijrar is NOT a specific/distinct mode - it is rather a REPEAT sale/purchase arrangement of normal sale, in which a seller agrees to sell various amounts/units of a commodity FROM TIME TO TIME. The seller may deliver the agreed commodity in a NUMBER OF CONSIGNMENTS, with price determined in advance, with EVERY consignment, or AFTER delivery of ALL consignments. The terms/conditions of the repeat sale may follow any NORMAL cash or credit sale structure. An agreement may exist between buyer and supplier for ongoing supply (e.g. monthly) of a particular product, at an agreed price and payment mode. This is a MODUS OPERANDI by which a MASTER AGREEMENT is signed for ongoing financing under any suitable normal mode. Because this is NORMAL day-to-day business, Shari'ah scholars give SOME RELAXATION on price fixation/payment timing, PROVIDED the arrangement does NOT involve Gharar. This type of arrangement typically occurs between WHOLESALERS and RETAILERS - normally the seller keeps delivering specified goods at a KNOWN price to the buyer, who makes payment monthly or as otherwise agreed (e.g. a bread supplier delivering to a retailer for a month, collecting payment once at month's end). HOWEVER, if any formal/informal contract under Istijrar is based on a SPECIFIC mode (like Murabaha or Salam), the CONDITIONS AND SHARI'AH ESSENTIALS OF THAT SPECIFIC MODE must still be fulfilled - e.g. for Murabaha, SEPARATE offer and acceptance is needed for EVERY consignment, based on the client's requisition each time.","examExplanation":"Istijrar = NOT a distinct mode; just a repeat-sale/supply arrangement using normal cash or credit sale terms, typically via a MASTER agreement for ongoing financing. Price can be fixed in advance, per consignment, or after all consignments deliver. Because it's normal day-to-day business (wholesaler-retailer relationships), Shari'ah scholars allow SOME relaxation on price fixation/payment timing, PROVIDED there's no Gharar. IMPORTANT CAVEAT: if Istijrar is structured via a SPECIFIC mode (e.g. Murabaha), that mode's FULL conditions still apply - e.g. Murabaha requires SEPARATE offer/acceptance for EACH consignment, based on the client's requisition each time.","keyPoints":["Istijrar is not a distinct financing mode - it is a repeat sale/purchase arrangement of a normal sale, where a seller agrees to supply various amounts of a commodity from time to time, typically via a master agreement.","Price can be determined in advance, with each individual consignment, or after delivery of all consignments; delivery can occur in multiple consignments over time.","Because Istijrar reflects normal day-to-day business (typically between wholesalers and retailers), Shari'ah scholars allow some relaxation on price fixation and payment timing, provided the arrangement does not involve Gharar.","Crucially, if an Istijrar arrangement is structured using a SPECIFIC mode (such as Murabaha), that mode's full conditions and Shari'ah essentials still apply - for example, Murabaha requires a separate offer and acceptance for every individual consignment, based on the client's requisition each time."],"definitions":[{"term":"Bai' al Istijrar","definition":"A repeat sale/purchase supply arrangement, not a distinct financing mode, in which a seller agrees to sell various amounts/units of a commodity to a buyer from time to time, typically under a master agreement for ongoing supply."}],"conditions":["An Istijrar arrangement must not involve Gharar to qualify for the relaxed treatment Shari'ah scholars allow on price fixation and payment timing.","If a specific mode (e.g. Murabaha) is used to structure an Istijrar arrangement, that mode's full conditions must still be fulfilled for every consignment - for Murabaha, this means a separate offer and acceptance for each delivery, based on the client's requisition."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Bread Supplier to Retailer","kind":"textbook","text":"A supplier of bread may go on supplying bread to a retailer for a month at a known price, and take payment once, after the month - a typical Istijrar arrangement reflecting normal day-to-day wholesaler-retailer business."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think Istijrar's relaxed price-fixation rules exempt it from ALL Shari'ah conditions. The chapter clarifies that if the arrangement is structured via a specific mode like Murabaha, that mode's own conditions (e.g. separate offer/acceptance per consignment) must still be fully satisfied."],"distinctions":["Istijrar as a generic repeat-sale arrangement (relaxed price/payment flexibility, provided no Gharar) vs Istijrar structured via a specific mode like Murabaha (that mode's full conditions, including per-consignment offer/acceptance, still apply)."],"relatedConcepts":["Murabaha","Salam","Gharar"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.5","pages":[355]},"transactionSteps":[{"step":"Buyer and Supplier Sign a Master Agreement","description":"The buyer and the supplier enter one master agreement covering an ongoing, repeated series of sales/purchases of a commodity, rather than negotiating a fresh contract for each delivery - a modus operandi for financing on an ongoing basis under any suitable normal (cash or credit) sale mode."},{"step":"Price-Fixing Method Is Agreed for the Series","description":"The parties agree how the price will be set for the whole arrangement: it may be determined in advance for the entire series, fixed with every individual consignment, or settled only after delivery of all consignments has been completed - whichever method they choose, the terms follow those of a normal cash or credit sale."},{"step":"Goods Delivered in Tranches per the Schedule","description":"The supplier goes on delivering the agreed goods in a number of consignments over time (e.g. monthly), as in the case of a bread supplier delivering to a retailer daily through the month, rather than in a single lump-sum delivery."},{"step":"Payment Settled per the Agreed Method","description":"The buyer pays according to whatever method was agreed - for example, once at the end of the month for all deliveries made during it, or on some other agreed schedule - reflecting normal day-to-day wholesaler-retailer business practice."},{"step":"Shari'ah Validity Requires No Gharar (and Any Specific Mode's Own Conditions Still Apply)","description":"Because Istijrar is normal, everyday business rather than a distinct financing mode, Shari'ah scholars allow some relaxation on exactly how and when price is fixed and paid, provided the arrangement does not involve Gharar (excessive uncertainty). However, if the master agreement is in fact structured through a specific mode such as Murabaha or Salam, that mode's own full conditions and Shari'ah essentials must still be fulfilled for every consignment - for Murabaha, this means a separate offer and acceptance is needed for each delivery, based on the client's requisition at that time."}]}]}],"questions":[{"id":"q13.1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which Wakalah type covers agency for purchase?","options":["Wakil-bil-Kusoomah","Wakil-bil-Shira","Wakil-bil-Bai‘","Wakil-bil-Taqazi al Dayn"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 347.","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"section":"13.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall Wakalah types","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[347,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.2","type":"tf","prompt":"In Wakalatul Istismar, the manager's fee depends on the profit of the fund.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"The fee is pre-agreed irrespective of profit or loss (p. 349).","topicId":"t13.2.2","chapter":13,"section":"13.2.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain investment agency","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2.2","pages":[349,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.3","type":"scenario","prompt":"A customer buys copper on credit from a bank and sells it in the market to an unrelated buyer for cash. This is:","options":["‘Inah","Permissible Tawarruq (preferred view)","Riba al-Fadl","Salam"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 349–350.","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"section":"13.3","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Classify Tawarruq","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.4","type":"multi","prompt":"Which Tawarruq arrangements are described as invalid? (Select all.)","options":["Bank appoints client as agent to buy and then sell to himself","Client's agency to the bank to sell is stipulated in the sale contract","Bank buys a liquid commodity and sells it to the client, who sells to a third party","Resale to the original credit seller"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,3],"explanation":"pp. 349–350.","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"section":"13.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Identify invalid structures","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.5","type":"identify","prompt":"A contract promising a reward to whoever recovers a stolen car is:","options":["Ijarah","Ju‘alah","Wakalah","Istijrar"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 351.","topicId":"t13.4","chapter":13,"section":"13.4","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify Ju‘alah","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4","pages":[351,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.6","type":"order","prompt":"Order the Ju‘alah process in Figure 13.1.","items":["Customer negotiates uncertain work with the bank","Bank signs Ju‘alah with the customer","Bank signs Parallel Ju‘alah with a worker","Worker completes work and is paid","Bank collects reward from customer"],"explanation":"pp. 353–354.","topicId":"t13.4.5","chapter":13,"section":"13.4.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Sequence Ju‘alah","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.5","pages":[353,354]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.7","type":"comparison","prompt":"Which statement correctly compares Ju‘alah and Ijarah of services?","options":["Both require specified work","In Ju‘alah the reward depends on achieving the result and the worker may be unspecified; in Ijarah work and worker are specified and wage is earned for time","Ijarah needs no acceptance","Ju‘alah is binding from the start"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 351–353.","topicId":"t13.4.3","chapter":13,"section":"13.4.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Compare contracts","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.3","pages":[353,353]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.8","type":"application","prompt":"A Ju‘alah pays 5% of debts recovered. The worker recovers Rs.2,000,000 of a Rs.5,000,000 portfolio before the deadline. Reward:","options":["Rs.250,000","Rs.100,000","Nothing","Rs.5,000"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"5% × 2,000,000 = 100,000 — reward may be proportionate to realised debts (p. 352). (Practice example — generated for learning.)","topicId":"t13.4.2","chapter":13,"section":"13.4.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Compute Ju‘alah reward","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.2","pages":[352,353]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.9","type":"short","prompt":"Why do scholars caution against extensive use of Tawarruq even when legally permissible?","keywords":["papers","cash","systemic","corporate","liquidity"],"correctAnswer":"Because organised Tawarruq can become an exchange of papers that delivers cash for a fixed return without real trade, resembling interest-based lending; it may lack conditions of valid sale (e.g. on commodity exchanges), creates systemic risk and harms the industry's credibility. Kahf would limit it to unavoidable corporate liquidity needs and exclude consumers; Siddiqi observes the client comes for cash and leaves with cash after signing papers.","explanation":"pp. 350–351.","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"section":"13.3","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Evaluate Tawarruq","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.10","type":"definition","prompt":"Bai‘ al Istijrar is best described as:","options":["A sale and buy-back","A repeat supply arrangement under a master agreement","A reward contract","An agency"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 355.","topicId":"t13.5","chapter":13,"section":"13.5","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define Istijrar","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.5","pages":[355,355]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.11","type":"tf","prompt":"An agent may appoint a sub-agent to do the job without the principal's consent.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"p. 348.","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"section":"13.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall agent duties","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[347,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.12","type":"match","prompt":"Match the contract with its key feature.","pairs":[["Wakalah","Agent acts as trustee for principal"],["Tawarruq","Credit purchase and spot sale to third party"],["Ju‘alah","Reward only on result"],["Istijrar","Repeat supply sale"]],"explanation":"Chapter 13.","topicId":"t13.1","chapter":13,"section":"13.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Match contracts","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.1","pages":[347,347]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.13","type":"mcq","prompt":"The word Tawkeel means:","options":["A guarantee of debt","Appointing someone to take charge of something, or delegating a job","A reward for a result","A sale on credit"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 347.","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"section":"13.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall Wakalah terminology","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[347,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.14","type":"match","prompt":"Match each Wakil type to its scope.","pairs":[["Wakil-bil-Kusoomah","Disputes and cases"],["Wakil-bil-Taqazi al Dayn","Receiving debt"],["Wakil-bil-Bai‘","Selling"],["Wakil-bil-Shira","Buying"]],"explanation":"p. 347.","topicId":"t13.2.1","chapter":13,"section":"13.2.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall types of Wakalah","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2.1","pages":[347,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.15","type":"multi","prompt":"For which acts is agency NOT permissible? (Select all.)","options":["Prayer","Giving evidence as an eyewitness","Sale and purchase","Taking an oath"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,3],"explanation":"These must be done personally (p. 348).","topicId":"t13.2.1","chapter":13,"section":"13.2.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify acts that admit representation","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2.1","pages":[347,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.16","type":"identify","prompt":"A bank asks an agent to sell its particular machine at a given price. This is a:","options":["General agency","Specific agency","Ju‘alah","Fuduli contract"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 348.","topicId":"t13.2.1","chapter":13,"section":"13.2.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish general and specific agency","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2.1","pages":[347,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.17","type":"tf","prompt":"On the preferred Shafi‘i and Hanbali view, goods an agent buys for his principal pass straight from the seller to the principal.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"They do not first enter the agent’s ownership (p. 348).","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"section":"13.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall transfer of ownership in agency","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[347,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.18","type":"scenario","prompt":"A purchasing agent supplies the principal with goods from his own warehouse without saying so. What rule has he broken?","options":["None","He must avoid conflicts of interest and fully disclose that the goods are his own","He needed a Kafalah","He should have charged a fee"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 348.","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"section":"13.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the agent’s duties","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[347,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.19","type":"mcq","prompt":"The preferred view on a contract made by a Fuduli is that it is:","options":["Void","Valid subject to ratification by the principal","Valid without ratification","Binding on the Fuduli only"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 348.","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"section":"13.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the Fuduli rule","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[347,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.20","type":"multi","prompt":"Which events end a Wakalah? (Select all.)","options":["Mutual agreement","Destruction of the subject matter","Death or loss of legal capacity","The agent earning a profit"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"Also unilateral termination and discharge of the obligation (p. 348).","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"section":"13.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall how Wakalah ends","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[347,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.21","type":"tf","prompt":"Islamic banks normally pay a fee to clients who buy goods on the bank’s behalf in Murabaha.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Banks mostly pay no fee to such clients, though they charge fees for agency services they provide (p. 349).","topicId":"t13.2.1","chapter":13,"section":"13.2.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall practice on agency fees","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2.1","pages":[347,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.22","type":"application","prompt":"A Wakalatul Istismar fund discloses a 3 % year-end fee on NAV. The NAV is 200 million after a loss year. What fee does the manager earn?","options":["0, because the fund lost money","6 million","3 million","Only a share of profit"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"The fee is independent of profit or loss: 3 % × 200 m (p. 349).","topicId":"t13.2.2","chapter":13,"section":"13.2.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Calculate a Wakalah fee","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2.2","pages":[349,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.23","type":"short","prompt":"How can a fund make every investor agree to the manager’s Wakalah fee basis?","keywords":["prospectus","before"],"correctAnswer":"Fix it before launch and disclose it in the prospectus; subscribers are presumed to accept its terms.","explanation":"p. 349.","topicId":"t13.2.2","chapter":13,"section":"13.2.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain disclosure of the Wakalah fee","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2.2","pages":[349,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.24","type":"comparison","prompt":"How did the Malikis view Tawarruq?","options":["As ‘Inah and forbidden","They saw no major problem and regarded it as a way to avoid Riba","As Makruh only for traders","They never discussed it"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Despite being strict about ‘Inah (p. 349).","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"section":"13.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Compare school views on Tawarruq","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.25","type":"identify","prompt":"Which scholars held Tawarruq impermissible?","options":["Ibnul-Hummam","Ibn Taymiyah and Ibn Qayyim","The majority of Hanbalis","The AAOIFI"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 349.","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"section":"13.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall opponents of Tawarruq","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.26","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank appoints the client as its agent to buy copper, and the client then sells the copper to himself on credit. Is this valid?","options":["Valid","Invalid — the purchase and sale are interdependent and the bank has taken neither possession nor risk","Valid but not advisable","Valid if the price is disclosed"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 350.","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"section":"13.3","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Evaluate organised Tawarruq structures","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.27","type":"scenario","prompt":"After an unconditional credit sale, the client separately appoints the bank to sell the commodity in the market for him. What is the ruling?","options":["Invalid","Valid but not advisable","Valid and recommended","Riba"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Had the agency been stipulated in the sale, it would be invalid (p. 350).","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"section":"13.3","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Evaluate organised Tawarruq structures","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.28","type":"tf","prompt":"According to Monzer Kahf, Tawarruq should be completely excluded from Islamic banking for individual consumers.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"p. 350.","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"section":"13.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall scholarly criticism of Tawarruq","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.29","type":"order","prompt":"Order the steps of an acceptable interbank Tawarruq.","items":["Both banks select a liquid commodity or blue-chip stock","The surplus bank buys it for cash in the market","The deficit bank buys it from the surplus bank on credit (Murabaha)","The deficit bank takes delivery and sells it at spot"],"explanation":"p. 351.","topicId":"t13.3.1","chapter":13,"section":"13.3.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Sequence interbank Tawarruq","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3.1","pages":[351,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.30","type":"mcq","prompt":"In Ju‘alah, the Ja‘il is:","options":["The worker","The offeror who promises the reward","The reward","The guarantor"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"The reward is the Ju‘l (p. 351).","topicId":"t13.4","chapter":13,"section":"13.4","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall Ju‘alah terminology","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4","pages":[351,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.31","type":"identify","prompt":"Which Qur’anic episode is cited as a basis for Ju‘alah?","options":["The story of Qarun","The reward of a camel-load of grain for finding the King’s beaker (Surah Yusuf)","The debt verse","The people of the cave"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 351.","topicId":"t13.4","chapter":13,"section":"13.4","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the basis of Ju‘alah","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4","pages":[351,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.32","type":"comparison","prompt":"Why is Ju‘alah suited to finding lost property when Ijarah is not?","options":["Ijarah is prohibited","Ijarah requires the work to be specified; Ju‘alah needs only a determined result","Ju‘alah pays more","Ijarah cannot involve banks"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 351.","topicId":"t13.4","chapter":13,"section":"13.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish Ju‘alah from Ijarah","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4","pages":[351,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.33","type":"scenario","prompt":"An offeror revokes a Ju‘alah after the worker has spent two weeks on the job. What is the worker entitled to?","options":["Nothing","A reasonable (market) wage for the work done","The full reward","Double the reward"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 352–353.","topicId":"t13.4.1","chapter":13,"section":"13.4.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply revocation rules in Ju‘alah","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.1","pages":[351,352]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.34","type":"tf","prompt":"A worker who abandons a Ju‘alah after starting can claim compensation for the effort he has put in.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"He has no claim unless agreed otherwise (p. 352).","topicId":"t13.4.1","chapter":13,"section":"13.4.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply revocation rules in Ju‘alah","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.1","pages":[351,352]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.35","type":"multi","prompt":"Which conditions apply to the reward in Ju‘alah? (Select all.)","options":["Known","Valuable (a permissible consideration)","Deliverable when required","Always a fixed lump sum"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"It may also be a share of the result (p. 352).","topicId":"t13.4.2","chapter":13,"section":"13.4.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall conditions of the reward","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.2","pages":[352,353]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.36","type":"identify","prompt":"Which task CANNOT be the subject of a Ju‘alah for the person named?","options":["A geologist finding a specified mineral","A debt collector recovering overdue debts","A court clerk filing a case as part of his official duties","A researcher preparing a report"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"The task must not be a legal or employment obligation of the worker (p. 352).","topicId":"t13.4.2","chapter":13,"section":"13.4.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Identify valid Ju‘alah tasks","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.2","pages":[352,353]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.37","type":"tf","prompt":"A Ju‘alah offered to the general public needs a counter-acceptance before it is concluded.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Counter-acceptance is not needed, except with a specified worker (p. 353).","topicId":"t13.4.3","chapter":13,"section":"13.4.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Recall formation of Ju‘alah","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.3","pages":[353,353]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.38","type":"multi","prompt":"In which cases is the worker entitled to the reward before completing the work? (Select all.)","options":["The property proves not to be the offeror’s and a legal decision says so","An accident, not caused by the worker’s negligence, impairs the subject matter","The worker asks for an advance","The deadline is near"],"correctAnswer":[0,1],"explanation":"In the accident case, the full reward (p. 353).","topicId":"t13.4.3","chapter":13,"section":"13.4.3","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall exceptions on entitlement","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.3","pages":[353,353]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.39","type":"short","prompt":"When may a bank that has taken on Ju‘alah work pass it to a third party under a Parallel Ju‘alah?","keywords":["itself","independent"],"correctAnswer":"When the first Ju‘alah does not require the bank to do the work itself; the two contracts must stay independent.","explanation":"p. 353.","topicId":"t13.4.4","chapter":13,"section":"13.4.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply Parallel Ju‘alah rules","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.4","pages":[353,353]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.40","type":"application","prompt":"A customer offers the bank 600,000 to recover goods; the bank hires a firm under Parallel Ju‘alah for 450,000. The firm succeeds. What is the bank’s margin?","options":["0","150,000","450,000","600,000"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"600,000 − 450,000 (pp. 353–354).","topicId":"t13.4.5","chapter":13,"section":"13.4.5","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Calculate the margin in Parallel Ju‘alah","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.5","pages":[353,354]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.41","type":"identify","prompt":"In Ju‘alah brokerage, when does the broker become entitled to the reward?","options":["On introducing the parties","On signing or completion of the contract he intermediated","Monthly","In advance"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 352, 355.","topicId":"t13.4.6","chapter":13,"section":"13.4.6","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the brokerage use of Ju‘alah","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.6","pages":[354,355]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.42","type":"tf","prompt":"Under Istijrar, the price may be fixed in advance, with each consignment, or after all deliveries.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"p. 355.","topicId":"t13.5","chapter":13,"section":"13.5","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall pricing in Istijrar","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.5","pages":[355,355]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q13.43","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank supplies a retailer monthly under an Istijrar master agreement using Murabaha. What is required for each consignment?","options":["Nothing beyond the master agreement","A separate offer and acceptance based on the client’s requisition","A new master agreement","Payment in advance"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 355.","topicId":"t13.5","chapter":13,"section":"13.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply Murabaha rules within Istijrar","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.5","pages":[355,355]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q-ch13-1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following acts CANNOT be delegated to an agent via Wakalah?","explanation":"Giving evidence/testimony must be performed by the principal himself and does not admit representation - an eyewitness cannot delegate his testimony to another person.","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"section":"13.2","topicLabel":"Wakalah (Agency): Definition, Types, and General Rules","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify which acts can and cannot be delegated to an agent.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[348]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Sale and purchase","Letting and hiring","Giving testimony as an eyewitness","Taking and making payments"],"correctAnswer":2},{"id":"q-ch13-2","type":"tf","prompt":"Per the preferred view (Shafi'i and Hanbali), when an agent purchases a commodity for his principal, ownership first passes to the agent before transferring to the principal.","explanation":"False - ownership transfers DIRECTLY from the seller to the principal, without first entering the agent's own ownership, per the preferred juristic view.","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"section":"13.2","topicLabel":"Wakalah (Agency): Definition, Types, and General Rules","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the general rules of Wakalah.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[348]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch13-3","type":"scenario","prompt":"A person concludes a contract on behalf of another without having proper authority to do so (a Fuduli). What is the status of this contract, per the preferred view?","explanation":"The preferred view holds that contracts concluded by a Fuduli (unauthorized person) are valid, subject to ratification by the principal.","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"section":"13.2","topicLabel":"Wakalah (Agency): Definition, Types, and General Rules","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Define Wakalah and explain unauthorized agency.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[348]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Automatically void with no remedy","Valid, subject to ratification by the principal","Automatically binding on the principal regardless of his wishes","Only valid if a court pre-approves it"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch13-4","type":"mcq","prompt":"How does a bank's compensation under Wakalatul Istismar differ from Mudarabah-based fund management?","explanation":"Under Wakalatul Istismar, the bank charges a pre-agreed fee irrespective of the fund's profit or loss, unlike Mudarabah where compensation is a share of actual profit.","topicId":"t13.2.2","chapter":13,"section":"13.2.2","topicLabel":"Wakalatul Istismar: Fund Management Agency","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain Wakalatul Istismar and its fee structure.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2.2","pages":[349]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Both are identical - profit-sharing in both cases","Wakalatul Istismar pays a pre-agreed fixed fee (lump sum or % of NAV) regardless of profit/loss; Mudarabah pays a profit share contingent on actual performance","Wakalatul Istismar always pays more than Mudarabah","Wakalatul Istismar cannot be used for fund management"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch13-5","type":"scenario","prompt":"A person buys a commodity on credit from Seller A, then resells it back to Seller A (the same party) for a lower cash price. What is this transaction called, and is it permissible?","explanation":"Reselling back to the SAME original seller (rather than a third party) is Bai' al 'Inah, which is not Shari'ah-compatible according to the majority view.","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"section":"13.3","topicLabel":"Tawarruq: Concept, Permissibility, and the Bai' al 'Inah Distinction","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain Tawarruq's distinction from the prohibited Bai' al 'Inah.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Tawarruq - permissible","Bai' al 'Inah - not permissible per the majority view","Ju'alah - permissible","Istijrar - permissible"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch13-6","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the preferred view across all four Fiqh schools regarding Tawarruq's permissibility?","explanation":"The preferred view in all four schools of Islamic Fiqh is that Tawarruq is permissible, though Qard al Hasan remains more preferable when available.","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"section":"13.3","topicLabel":"Tawarruq: Concept, Permissibility, and the Bai' al 'Inah Distinction","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain Tawarruq's permissibility across the four schools.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[350]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["It is unanimously prohibited","It is permissible, though Qard al Hasan (interest-free lending) is more preferable where available","Only the Hanafi school permits it","It is permissible only for governments, never for banks"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch13-7","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank appoints a customer (Mutawarriq) as its agent to purchase a commodity on the bank's behalf, and the SAME agency agreement also has him sell that commodity to himself. What is the Shari'ah status of this transaction?","explanation":"The chapter explicitly states this structure is invalid, since the two transactions are interdependent and the bank does not take genuine possession/business risk.","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"section":"13.3","topicLabel":"Tawarruq: Concept, Permissibility, and the Bai' al 'Inah Distinction","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Identify structuring pitfalls that can invalidate Tawarruq.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[350]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Valid, since agency is always permissible","Invalid - the purchase and sale become interdependent, and the bank never genuinely takes possession or business risk","Valid, provided the customer pays a fee","Valid only if approved by a court"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch13-8","type":"short","prompt":"What caution does the chapter raise about widespread use of Tawarruq, citing Monzer Kahf and Nejatullah Siddiqi?","explanation":"This reflects the scholarly cautions cited in section 13.3.","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"section":"13.3","topicLabel":"Tawarruq: Concept, Permissibility, and the Bai' al 'Inah Distinction","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain why scholars urge caution about Tawarruq's widespread use.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[350]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Monzer Kahf states Tawarruq must be completely out of Islamic banking practices for individual consumers. Nejatullah Siddiqi criticizes it as reducing to the client signing a number of papers to get cash and collateral. The author warns this wide, careless use creates systemic risk for the nascent Islamic finance industry."},{"id":"q-ch13-9","type":"mcq","prompt":"Arrange the steps of a genuine inter-bank liquidity Tawarruq: (A) Deficit bank resells the commodity at spot price for cash; (B) Surplus bank purchases a liquid commodity/stock for cash; (C) Deficit bank purchases the commodity from the surplus bank on credit (Murabaha).","explanation":"Correct order: surplus bank buys for cash (B) -> sells to deficit bank on credit/Murabaha (C) -> deficit bank resells at spot for cash (A).","topicId":"t13.3.1","chapter":13,"section":"13.3.1","topicLabel":"Genuine Tawarruq for Inter-bank Liquidity Management","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Describe how genuine Tawarruq arrangements are used for inter-bank liquidity management.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3.1","pages":[351]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"ordering","options":["B, C, A","C, B, A","A, B, C","B, A, C"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch13-10","type":"tf","prompt":"The author considers Tawarruq an appropriate routine, everyday liquidity management tool for Islamic banks.","explanation":"False - the author states Tawarruq should be used only in extreme cases where no alternative exists to avoid interest, and that widespread use is harmful to the industry in the long run.","topicId":"t13.3.1","chapter":13,"section":"13.3.1","topicLabel":"Genuine Tawarruq for Inter-bank Liquidity Management","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain why widespread Tawarruq use is discouraged.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3.1","pages":[351]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch13-11","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the key structural difference between Ijarah and Ju'alah?","explanation":"Ijarah requires the work itself to be precisely specified upfront; Ju'alah only requires the required end result to be determined, making it suitable for uncertain-outcome tasks.","topicId":"t13.4","chapter":13,"section":"13.4","topicLabel":"Ju'alah: Concept, Permissibility, and Distinction from Ijarah","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish Ju'alah from Ijarah.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4","pages":[351]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Ijarah requires the work to be precisely specified; Ju'alah requires only the end result to be determined","Ju'alah requires the work to be precisely specified; Ijarah requires only the end result","There is no meaningful difference between the two","Ju'alah can never be used by Islamic banks"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch13-12","type":"identify","prompt":"Which Qur'anic Surah is cited as textual authority for Ju'alah's permissibility?","explanation":"Surah Yousuf references the King's lost beaker, with a reward of a camel-load of grain offered to whoever finds it - cited as Qur'anic authority for Ju'alah.","topicId":"t13.4","chapter":13,"section":"13.4","topicLabel":"Ju'alah: Concept, Permissibility, and Distinction from Ijarah","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain Ju'alah's Qur'anic and Prophetic basis.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4","pages":[351]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Surah Al-Baqarah","Surah Yousuf","Surah Al-Fatiha","Surah An-Nisa"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch13-13","type":"scenario","prompt":"A worker in a Ju'alah contract spends significant effort attempting to locate lost property but ultimately fails. What compensation is he entitled to?","explanation":"Payment is strictly conditional on actually realizing the required result - a worker who genuinely attempts but fails receives no compensation for time/effort.","topicId":"t13.4.1","chapter":13,"section":"13.4.1","topicLabel":"Parties, Binding Nature, and Termination of Ju'alah","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain the binding nature and payment rules of Ju'alah.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.1","pages":[351,352]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Full reward, since he made a genuine effort","None - realization of the result is necessary for compensation; effort alone, without success, is not compensated","Half the agreed reward","A court-determined amount based on hours worked"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch13-14","type":"mcq","prompt":"If an offeror revokes a Ju'alah contract AFTER the worker has already commenced work, what must the offeror do?","explanation":"Once the worker commences work, the contract becomes binding; if the offeror revokes at that point, he must give the worker a reasonable wage, since the work performed is legally valid and should not cause the worker loss.","topicId":"t13.4.1","chapter":13,"section":"13.4.1","topicLabel":"Parties, Binding Nature, and Termination of Ju'alah","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the termination rules of Ju'alah.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.1","pages":[352]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Nothing - Ju'alah is never binding","Pay the worker a reasonable wage for work already performed","Pay the worker the full original reward regardless of outcome","Pay double the original reward as a penalty"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch13-15","type":"tf","prompt":"A Ju'alah reward can be structured as a percentage of the realized result, such as a share of collected debt.","explanation":"True - the reward can also be a certain portion of the realized result, as in debt-collection Ju'alah where the reward is a percentage of the amount collected.","topicId":"t13.4.2","chapter":13,"section":"13.4.2","topicLabel":"Subject Matter, Reward Rules, and Execution of Ju'alah","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the reward rules of Ju'alah.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.2","pages":[352]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":true},{"id":"q-ch13-16","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why does concluding a Ju'alah contract not require counter-acceptance, unlike Ijarah?","explanation":"The requirement of counter-acceptance in Ju'alah is practically unattainable for an open public offer, so the contract is concluded without it (except when a specific worker is named).","topicId":"t13.4.3","chapter":13,"section":"13.4.3","topicLabel":"Subject Matter, Reward Rules, and Execution of Ju'alah","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the execution rules of a Ju'alah contract.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.3","pages":[353]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Because Ju'alah contracts are never legally binding","Because counter-acceptance is practically unattainable for an open offer to the general public - performing the work itself constitutes acceptance","Because Ju'alah always involves a written contract","Because only banks can be offerors in Ju'alah"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch13-17","type":"scenario","prompt":"A worker's Ju'alah project (restoring a piece of equipment) is damaged by an accident that was not due to his negligence, before completion. What is his entitlement?","explanation":"When an accident not due to the worker's negligence/misconduct impairs the subject matter's value, the worker is entitled to the full reward - one of the two exceptions to the completion requirement.","topicId":"t13.4.3","chapter":13,"section":"13.4.3","topicLabel":"Subject Matter, Reward Rules, and Execution of Ju'alah","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain the exceptions to Ju'alah's completion-based payment rule.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.3","pages":[353]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["No entitlement, since the work was not completed","He is entitled to the FULL reward, since the impairment resulted from a non-negligent accident","He must repay any advance received","He is entitled to only half the reward"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch13-18","type":"short","prompt":"Describe the five-step process Islamic banks use for Ju'alah with a Parallel Ju'alah structure.","explanation":"This matches the five-step process described in section 13.4.5.","topicId":"t13.4.4","chapter":13,"section":"13.4.4","topicLabel":"Parallel Ju'alah, the Bank's Practical Process, and Ju'alah-Based Products","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Walk through the practical bank process for Ju'alah, including Parallel Ju'alah.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.5","pages":[353,354]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"(1) The customer negotiates with the bank for performance of uncertain work in a specified time for an agreed reward. (2) The bank agrees after a cost-benefit analysis, and a Ju'alah contract is signed. (3) The bank finds a worker with relevant expertise and signs a Parallel Ju'alah contract with him. (4) The worker completes the work and is paid by the bank. (5) The bank collects its own reward from the original customer."},{"id":"q-ch13-19","type":"mcq","prompt":"In a debt-collection Ju'alah where an advance reward payment is made before the debt is fully collected, how is that advance treated?","explanation":"The reward can be paid in advance, in full or part, before work completion, but the worker is not absolutely entitled until the result is realized - so the payment is treated as 'on account'.","topicId":"t13.4.6","chapter":13,"section":"13.4.6","topicLabel":"Parallel Ju'alah, the Bank's Practical Process, and Ju'alah-Based Products","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Identify real financial products built on Ju'alah.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.6","pages":[354,355]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["As the worker's fully secured, non-refundable income","As an 'on account' payment, since the worker is not absolutely entitled until the required result is realized","As a loan the worker must repay with interest","As a violation of Shari'ah, since advances are never allowed"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch13-20","type":"tf","prompt":"Bai' al Istijrar is a distinct, separate financing mode with its own unique set of Shari'ah rules.","explanation":"False - Istijrar is not a specific mode; it is a repeat sale/purchase arrangement of normal sale, using whichever underlying sale terms (cash or credit) apply.","topicId":"t13.5","chapter":13,"section":"13.5","topicLabel":"Bai' al Istijrar: The Repeat-Sale Supply Contract","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain Bai' al Istijrar and its conditions.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.5","pages":[355]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch13-21","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank structures an ongoing Istijrar supply arrangement with a client using a Murabaha mode for each delivery. What additional requirement applies compared to a generic Istijrar arrangement?","explanation":"If the Istijrar arrangement is based on a specific mode like Murabaha, that mode's conditions and Shari'ah essentials must be fulfilled - meaning separate offer and acceptance for every consignment.","topicId":"t13.5","chapter":13,"section":"13.5","topicLabel":"Bai' al Istijrar: The Repeat-Sale Supply Contract","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain the conditions under which repeat-sale arrangements are Shari'ah-compliant.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.5","pages":[355]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["No additional requirements apply","A separate offer and acceptance is needed for every individual consignment, based on the client's requisition each time","Only one offer and acceptance is needed for the entire master agreement","Murabaha cannot be used within an Istijrar arrangement at all"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch13-22","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why do Shari'ah scholars give some relaxation on price fixation and payment timing for Istijrar arrangements?","explanation":"Since Istijrar is normal day-to-day business, Shari'ah scholars give some relaxation on price fixation/payment as long as the arrangement does not involve Gharar.","topicId":"t13.5","chapter":13,"section":"13.5","topicLabel":"Bai' al Istijrar: The Repeat-Sale Supply Contract","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain Bai' al Istijrar and its conditions.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.5","pages":[355]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Because Istijrar is exempt from all Shari'ah rules","Because it reflects normal, everyday business (e.g. wholesaler-retailer relationships), provided the arrangement does not involve Gharar","Because Istijrar always involves government parties","Because Istijrar contracts are never enforceable"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch13-23","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following are among the five types of Wakalah listed in Chapter 13?","explanation":"The five types are Wakil-bil-Kusoomah, Wakil-bil-Taqazi al Dayn, Wakil-bil-Qabaza al Dayn, Wakil-bil-Bai', and Wakil-bil-Shira. 'Wakil-bil-Riba' and 'Wakil-bil-Zakat' are not among them (Riba-based business cannot even be the subject of a valid agency).","topicId":"t13.2.1","chapter":13,"section":"13.2.1","topicLabel":"Wakalah (Agency): Definition, Types, and General Rules","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define Wakalah and identify which acts can and cannot be delegated to an agent.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2.1","pages":[347]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Wakil-bil-Kusoomah (disputes/cases)","Wakil-bil-Riba (interest agency)","Wakil-bil-Bai' (trading agency)","Wakil-bil-Shira (purchase agency)","Wakil-bil-Zakat (Zakat agency)"],"correctAnswer":[0,2,3]},{"id":"q-ch13-24","type":"short","prompt":"What example from the Prophet's (pbuh) own life is cited as textual evidence for Wakalah's permissibility?","explanation":"This is cited, along with Caliph Ali and other Companions delegating their business to others, as foundational textual evidence for agency's permissibility.","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"section":"13.2","topicLabel":"Wakalah (Agency): Definition, Types, and General Rules","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define Wakalah and identify which acts can and cannot be delegated to an agent.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[347]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"The Prophet (pbuh) delegated the job of purchasing a goat for him to a Companion named 'Urwah al Barqi."},{"id":"q-ch13-25","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank appoints an agent with instructions to sell one specific piece of land the bank owns at a stated price. Is this a specific or general agency contract?","explanation":"A specific agency contract involves one particular asset/task per a given instruction (e.g. selling one specific asset at a stated price); a general agency contract covers a recurring category of tasks (e.g. purchasing certain kinds of goods as and when asked).","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"section":"13.2","topicLabel":"Wakalah (Agency): Definition, Types, and General Rules","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Identify which acts can and cannot be delegated to an agent.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[348]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Specific agency - a particular asset is being sold per a given instruction","General agency - because any sale is a recurring activity","Neither - this cannot be delegated via Wakalah","It is a Ju'alah contract, not Wakalah"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch13-26","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following contracts is attributed explicitly to the principal only, even though agency for it is otherwise permitted?","explanation":"Marriage, divorce and murder settlement are attributed explicitly to the principal, unlike sale, hiring, and debt assignment, where rights/liabilities (delivery, price claims, being sued for non-performance) attach to the agent.","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"section":"13.2","topicLabel":"Wakalah (Agency): Definition, Types, and General Rules","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Identify which acts can and cannot be delegated to an agent.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[348]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Sale of goods","Marriage","Letting/hiring","Assignment of debt"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch13-27","type":"tf","prompt":"Tawarruq conducted through a national or international commodity exchange, where only brokers perform agency services and goods never actually change ownership, is considered fully safe from Shari'ah concerns.","explanation":"False - such exchange-based Tawarruq is vulnerable to violation of Shari'ah rules, since several conditions of a valid sale (including genuine transfer of ownership) may be lacking.","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"section":"13.3","topicLabel":"Tawarruq: Concept, Permissibility, and the Bai' al 'Inah Distinction","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain Tawarruq's structure, its distinction from the prohibited Bai' al 'Inah, and why scholars urge caution about its widespread use.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[350]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch13-28","type":"scenario","prompt":"A Ju'alah worker is holding the offeror's property while attempting to complete the job, and the property is damaged due to the worker's own carelessness. Is the worker liable?","explanation":"The worker is considered a trustee (Amin) regarding the offeror's property in his possession, and is liable for loss only in cases of negligence, misconduct, or violation of stipulated conditions.","topicId":"t13.4.1","chapter":13,"section":"13.4.1","topicLabel":"Parties, Binding Nature, and Termination of Ju'alah","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain Ju'alah, distinguish it from Ijarah, and explain why it suits tasks with an uncertain outcome or unspecifiable work.","source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.1","pages":[352]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["No - a Ju'alah worker is never liable for any loss","Yes - a trustee is liable for loss caused by his own negligence or misconduct","Yes, but only if the offeror sues within 24 hours","No - liability only ever applies to the offeror"],"correctAnswer":1}],"flashcards":[{"id":"f13.1","front":"Wakalah","back":"Agency — appointing another to act for oneself; the Wakil discharges duties as a trustee (Amanah).","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[347,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.2","front":"Fuduli","back":"Unauthorised person acting for another; contract valid subject to principal's ratification (preferred view).","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[347,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.3","front":"Acts that cannot be delegated by Wakalah","back":"Personal acts such as prayer, fasting, giving evidence, taking an oath; and prohibited acts (theft, Riba business).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[347,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.4","front":"Wakalatul Istismar","back":"Fund management as agent for a pre-agreed fee (lump sum or % of investment/NAV) regardless of profit or loss; basis disclosed before launch.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t13.2.2","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2.2","pages":[349,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.5","front":"Tawarruq","back":"Buying on credit and selling at spot to a third party to obtain cash.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.6","front":"Tawarruq vs ‘Inah","back":"Tawarruq: sold to third party — permissible (preferred view, AAOIFI). ‘Inah: sold back to original seller — invalid.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.7","front":"Invalid organised Tawarruq structures","back":"Bank appoints client agent to buy and then sells to himself; sale-agency to the bank stipulated as a condition of the sale.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.8","front":"Scholars' caution on Tawarruq","back":"Kahf: corporate unavoidable liquidity only, never consumers. Siddiqi: ‘comes wanting cash, leaves with cash after signing papers’.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.9","front":"Interbank Tawarruq (Commodity Murabaha)","back":"Surplus bank buys liquid commodity for cash; deficit bank buys on deferred Murabaha, takes delivery, sells spot.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t13.3.1","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3.1","pages":[351,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.10","front":"Ju‘alah","back":"Offeror (Ja‘il) promises known reward (Ju‘l) to whoever achieves a specified result; no result, no reward.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t13.4","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4","pages":[351,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.11","front":"Ju‘alah termination","back":"Before work: no reward. Offeror revokes after work starts: reasonable/market wage. Worker revokes after starting: no claim unless agreed.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t13.4.1","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.1","pages":[351,352]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.12","front":"Ju‘l vs Ujrah","back":"Ju‘l due only on result; Ujrah earned for time worked even if task incomplete.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t13.4.3","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.3","pages":[353,353]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.13","front":"Ju‘alah products","back":"Debt collection (% of recoveries; advances on account), securing permissible financing, brokerage.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t13.4.6","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.6","pages":[354,355]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.14","front":"Bai‘ al Istijrar","back":"Repeat supply sale under a master agreement; price relaxations absent Gharar; Murabaha consignments need separate offer/acceptance.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t13.5","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.5","pages":[355,355]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.15","front":"Tawkeel","back":"Appointing someone to take charge of something, or delegating a job (same root as Wakalah) (p. 347).","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[347,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.16","front":"Agent’s core duties","back":"Follow instructions, use due care and skill, no sub-agent without consent, and avoid conflicts of interest, e.g. disclose if he sells his own goods (p. 348).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[347,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.17","front":"How Wakalah ends","back":"Mutual agreement, unilateral termination, discharge of the obligation, destruction of the subject matter, death or loss of capacity (p. 348).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[347,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.18","front":"General vs specific agency","back":"General: buy certain goods whenever asked. Specific: sell a particular asset at a given price. The job must be clearly defined in both (p. 348).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t13.2.1","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2.1","pages":[347,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.19","front":"Mutawarriq","back":"The person who obtains cash through Tawarruq (p. 349).","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.20","front":"School views on Tawarruq","back":"Hanbali majority, Shafi‘is, Malikis and the Hanafi majority (Ibnul-Hummam) permit it. Ibn Taymiyah and Ibn Qayyim forbid it. Qard Hasan is preferable (pp. 349–350).","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.21","front":"Ja‘il and Ju‘l","back":"Ja‘il: the offeror of the reward. Ju‘l: the reward itself (p. 351).","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t13.4","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4","pages":[351,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.22","front":"Revocation in Ju‘alah","back":"Before work: no reward. By the offeror after work starts: a reasonable market wage. By the worker after starting: no claim unless agreed (pp. 352–353).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t13.4.1","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.1","pages":[351,352]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.23","front":"Ju‘alah task conditions","back":"It must involve effort and must not already be the worker’s legal or employment duty. The result must be determined (p. 352).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t13.4.2","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.2","pages":[352,353]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.24","front":"Early entitlement to the Ju‘alah reward","back":"(1) The property proves not to be the offeror’s (legal decision); (2) a non-negligent accident impairs the subject matter, giving the full reward (p. 353).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t13.4.3","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.3","pages":[353,353]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.25","front":"Ju‘alah process (Figure 13.1)","back":"Negotiation → bank signs Ju‘alah → Parallel Ju‘alah with a worker → worker completes and is paid → bank collects its reward (pp. 353–354).","category":"Banking","topicId":"t13.4.5","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.5","pages":[353,354]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f13.26","front":"Istijrar with Murabaha","back":"The master agreement is allowed, but each consignment needs separate offer and acceptance (p. 355).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t13.5","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.5","pages":[355,355]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-1","front":"What is Wakalah, and what responsibility does a Wakil (agent) hold?","back":"Wakalah is agency - appointing someone to look after or act on your behalf. A Wakil must discharge his responsibility the way a trustee discharges Amanah.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[347]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-2","front":"What acts CANNOT be delegated via Wakalah?","back":"Personal religious/legal acts that must be performed by the principal himself - prayer, fasting, giving testimony, and taking an oath - because these acts do not 'admit representation.'","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[348]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-3","front":"Per the preferred juristic view (Shafi'i and Hanbali), when an agent purchases something for his principal, whose ownership does it pass through?","back":"It transfers DIRECTLY from the seller to the principal, without first passing through the agent's own ownership.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[348]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-4","front":"What is a Fuduli, and what is the status of contracts he concludes?","back":"A person who undertakes a job/contract on behalf of another without proper authority. Per the preferred view, such contracts are VALID, subject to the principal's subsequent ratification.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[348]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-5","front":"What is Wakalatul Istismar, and how does its fee structure differ from Mudarabah?","back":"Investment agency fund management - the bank earns a PRE-AGREED FIXED fee (lump sum or % of NAV) regardless of the fund's profit or loss, unlike Mudarabah's profit-sharing model.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t13.2.2","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2.2","pages":[349]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-6","front":"What is Tawarruq?","back":"Buying a commodity on credit and immediately reselling it (to a third party) for spot cash, purely to generate liquidity - the commodity itself is not the buyer's actual need.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-7","front":"How does Tawarruq differ from Bai' al 'Inah?","back":"In Tawarruq, the Mutawarriq sells the commodity to a genuine THIRD PARTY. In 'Inah, the buyer resells it back to the SAME original seller with a price differential - which is prohibited.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-8","front":"What is the preferred view across all four Fiqh schools (Hanafi, Maliki, Shafi'e, Hanbali) on Tawarruq's permissibility?","back":"The preferred view in all four schools is that Tawarruq is permissible - though Qard al Hasan (interest-free lending) remains more preferable where available.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[350]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-9","front":"Why is it invalid if a bank appoints the Mutawarriq as its agent to both purchase AND sell a commodity to himself?","back":"The two transactions (purchase and sale) become interdependent, and the bank never genuinely takes possession or business risk.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[350]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-10","front":"What is Monzer Kahf's view on Tawarruq for individual consumers?","back":"\"For individual consumers it must be completely out of the Islamic banking practices.\"","category":"Risk","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[350]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-11","front":"Describe the basic structure of inter-bank liquidity Tawarruq (Commodity Murabaha).","back":"The surplus bank buys a liquid commodity/stock for cash; sells it to the deficit bank on credit (Murabaha structure); the deficit bank takes delivery and immediately resells it at spot price in the market for cash.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t13.3.1","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3.1","pages":[351]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-12","front":"What is Ju'alah?","back":"A contract where one party (the Ja'il, offeror) promises a specific reward (the Jua'l) to anyone able to realize a specific or uncertain required result.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t13.4","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4","pages":[351]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-13","front":"What is the key difference between Ijarah and Ju'alah regarding specification requirements?","back":"Ijarah requires the WORK itself to be precisely specified upfront. Ju'alah only requires the required END RESULT to be determined - making it suited to uncertain-outcome tasks Ijarah cannot handle.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t13.4","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4","pages":[351]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-14","front":"What Qur'anic reference supports Ju'alah's permissibility?","back":"Surah Yousuf's account of the King's lost beaker - whoever found it would receive a camel-load of grain as reward.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t13.4","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4","pages":[351]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-15","front":"Does a Ju'alah worker get paid if he tries hard but fails to achieve the required result?","back":"No - realization of the end result is necessary for compensation. A worker who genuinely attempts but fails receives nothing for his effort or time spent.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t13.4.1","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.1","pages":[351,352]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-16","front":"When does a Ju'alah contract become binding, and what happens if the offeror revokes after that point?","back":"It becomes binding once the worker COMMENCES work. If the offeror revokes at that point, he must pay the worker a reasonable wage (not the full reward).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t13.4.1","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.1","pages":[352]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-17","front":"Does concluding a Ju'alah contract require counter-acceptance, unlike Ijarah?","back":"No - counter-acceptance is not required (unlike Ijarah), since it is practically unattainable for an open public offer; performing the work itself constitutes acceptance (except with a specified worker).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t13.4.3","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.3","pages":[353]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-18","front":"How does the payment-timing rule differ between Ujrah and Ju'alah?","back":"In Ujrah, a worker who agreed to work a stipulated TIME is paid once that time is worked, regardless of whether the work is finished. In Ju'alah, the reward is NOT enforceable until the required work is actually COMPLETED.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t13.4.3","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.3","pages":[353]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-19","front":"Name the two situations where a Ju'alah worker is entitled to reward BEFORE the work is completed.","back":"(1) The worker is found to have worked on property not belonging to the offeror (per legal decision); (2) a non-negligent accident impairs the subject matter's value - the worker gets the FULL reward in this case.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t13.4.3","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.3","pages":[353]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-20","front":"What is Parallel Ju'alah?","back":"A structure where a bank takes on a Ju'alah job as 'worker' (from a customer), then subcontracts the actual work via a separate, independent Parallel Ju'alah contract with a genuine third-party expert.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t13.4.4","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.4","pages":[353]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-21","front":"In debt-collection Ju'alah, how is the reward typically structured, and what happens with advance payment?","back":"The reward is typically a percentage of the amount collected. Advance payment is possible, but treated as 'on account,' since the worker is not absolutely entitled until the result (collection) is realized.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t13.4.6","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.6","pages":[354,355]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-22","front":"Is Bai' al Istijrar a distinct financing mode?","back":"No - it is a repeat sale/purchase arrangement of a normal sale, where a seller agrees to supply various amounts of a commodity over time, typically via a master agreement.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t13.5","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.5","pages":[355]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-23","front":"If an Istijrar arrangement is structured via Murabaha, what additional requirement applies to each consignment?","back":"A separate offer and acceptance is needed for every consignment, based on the client's requisition each time - Murabaha's full conditions still apply.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t13.5","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.5","pages":[355]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-24","front":"List the five types of Wakalah described in Chapter 13.","back":"Wakil-bil-Kusoomah (taking up disputes/cases), Wakil-bil-Taqazi al Dayn (receiving debt), Wakil-bil-Qabaza al Dayn (possession of debt), Wakil-bil-Bai' (agency for trading), and Wakil-bil-Shira (agency for purchase).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t13.2.1","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2.1","pages":[347]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-25","front":"What textual evidence from the Prophet's (pbuh) own practice supports the permissibility of Wakalah?","back":"The Prophet (pbuh) himself delegated the job of purchasing a goat for him to a Companion named 'Urwah al Barqi; the fourth Caliph Ali and other Companions also delegated their business to others.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[347]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-26","front":"Distinguish a specific agency contract from a general agency contract, with an example of each.","back":"Specific: a bank asks an agent to sell one particular asset at a given price/instruction. General: a bank appoints an agent to purchase certain kinds of goods as and when asked. Even in a general contract, the job's nature must be clearly defined to avoid disputes.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[348]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-27","front":"Which contracts are attributed explicitly to the principal only, even though the agent handles most rights/liabilities in a transaction?","back":"Marriage, divorce, and settlement of murder are attributed explicitly to the principal - unlike delivery, price claims, inspection rights, and being sued for non-performance, which are attributed to the agent.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[348]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-28","front":"Why is Tawarruq carried out through national/international commodity exchanges vulnerable to Shari'ah violations?","back":"Because only brokers perform nominal agency services and the goods always remain where they were, without any real transfer of ownership from seller to buyer - several conditions of a valid sale may be lacking.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[350]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-29","front":"Besides simple commodity purchase/resale, what other structures do some Islamic banks use to conduct Tawarruq, and what caution applies?","back":"Shares of joint stock companies, Ijarah Sukuk (tangible assets and services), and 'bundles of assets' (real assets plus cash/receivables, with real assets forming the majority). Despite legal permissibility, extensive use of such financing needs to be avoided.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[350]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-30","front":"Can a Ju'alah worker be unspecified, and how does this differ from Ijarah?","back":"Yes - the worker may be any specified person(s) or simply the general public (unlike Ijarah). An unspecified public offer lets any interested person attempt the work, alone or with help; a specified worker must personally undertake it, or involve others only with the offeror's express consent.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t13.4.1","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.1","pages":[351,352]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch13-31","front":"What is a Ju'alah worker's liability status regarding the offeror's property in his possession?","back":"The worker is considered a trustee (Amin) regarding any offeror property in his possession - not liable for loss except in cases of negligence, misconduct, or violation of the stipulated conditions.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t13.4.1","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.1","pages":[352]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}],"exam":[{"id":"e13.1","kind":"long","prompt":"Critically evaluate Tawarruq as practised by Islamic banks.","expectedStructure":["Definition and ‘Inah distinction","Juristic views of four schools and AAOIFI","Valid, invalid and ‘not advisable’ structures","Commodity-exchange Tawarruq","Scholarly cautions (Kahf, Siddiqi)","Interbank liquidity use and safeguards"],"keyConcepts":["Mutawarriq","‘Inah","organised Tawarruq"],"essentialPoints":["Qard al Hasan preferable"],"commonMistakes":["Calling all Tawarruq ‘Inah","Ignoring agency structures"],"topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e13.2","kind":"short","prompt":"Explain Ju‘alah and how an Islamic bank can use it for debt recovery.","expectedStructure":["Definition and legitimacy","Parties and reward","Termination rules","Parallel Ju‘alah and Figure 13.1","Debt-collection product"],"keyConcepts":["Ju‘l","result"],"essentialPoints":["Reward as % of recovery"],"commonMistakes":["Treating reward as due for effort"],"topicId":"t13.4","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4","pages":[351,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e13.3","kind":"conceptual","prompt":"Discuss the rules of Wakalah and its role in Islamic banking products.","expectedStructure":["Meaning and legitimacy","Types","Subject matter and duties","Ownership transfer and Fuduli","Termination","Uses and Wakalatul Istismar"],"keyConcepts":["Amanah","Wakil"],"essentialPoints":["Negligence clause"],"commonMistakes":["Letting agent bear business risk"],"topicId":"t13.2","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2","pages":[347,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e13.4","kind":"viva","prompt":"How does a fund manager under Wakalatul Istismar differ from a Mudarib?","expectedStructure":["Fee vs profit share","Who bears loss","Disclosure"],"keyConcepts":["NAV fee"],"essentialPoints":["Prospectus disclosure"],"commonMistakes":["Saying Wakil shares losses"],"topicId":"t13.2.2","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.2.2","pages":[349,349]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e13.5","kind":"difference","prompt":"Distinguish Ju‘alah from Ijarah of services.","expectedStructure":["Specification of work","Parties","Acceptance","Entitlement","Binding nature"],"keyConcepts":["Ju‘l","Ujrah","Result"],"essentialPoints":["Ijarah needs specified work; Ju‘alah only a determined result (p. 351)","Ju‘alah worker may be the public (p. 351)","No counter-acceptance needed for a public offer (p. 353)","Reward only on completion; wage for time in Ijarah (p. 353)","Ju‘alah not binding until work begins (p. 352)"],"commonMistakes":["Saying Ju‘alah requires known work","Ignoring the exceptions for early entitlement"],"topicId":"t13.4.3","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.4.3","pages":[353,353]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e13.6","kind":"scenario","prompt":"A client asks your bank for cash to meet a personal need; the product team proposes Tawarruq through a commodity exchange. Advise the Shari’ah board.","expectedStructure":["Define Tawarruq and ‘Inah","Juristic views","Structural tests","Commodity exchange risk","Policy advice"],"keyConcepts":["Mutawarriq","Organised Tawarruq","Qard Hasan"],"essentialPoints":["A sale to a third party is acceptable; a sale back is ‘Inah (pp. 349–350)","Agency to sell stipulated in the sale is invalid (p. 350)","Exchanges where goods never move are vulnerable (p. 350)","Kahf: exclude it for consumers; Siddiqi’s criticism (p. 350)","Limit it to unavoidable corporate liquidity (p. 350)"],"commonMistakes":["Treating legal validity as sufficient","Ignoring possession and risk"],"topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349,351]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-25","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"Explain the structure of Tawarruq, the Shari'ah condition distinguishing it from Bai' al-'Inah, and why its use is controversial.","expectedStructure":["Define Tawarruq (buy on credit, sell at spot for cash; the need is liquidity, not the commodity)","Distinguish it from Bai' al-'Inah (resale to a third party vs resale to the same seller)","State the majority permissibility view across the four schools, subject to conditions","Explain the practical/Shari'ah controversy: agency structures that collapse the two legs, exchange-based Tawarruq, and scholarly warnings against extensive/individual use"],"keyConcepts":["Tawarruq","Bai' al-'Inah","Mutawarriq","Liquidity management"],"essentialPoints":["Tawarruq means buying a commodity on credit and immediately selling it (to a third party) for cash, purely to obtain liquidity — the commodity itself is not the buyer's real need.","The key distinction from Bai' al-'Inah is who buys back the commodity: in Tawarruq it is resold to a third party, while in 'Inah it is resold back to the original seller at a different price; the latter is far more widely regarded as a Riba-avoidance ruse.","The preferred view across all four schools of Fiqh is that Tawarruq is permissible (Hanbali and Shafi'i jurists generally allow it; the majority Hanafi and Maliki positions allow it when resale is genuinely to the market), and AAOIFI holds that resale to the original seller, directly or via an agent, is invalid while resale to a third party is acceptable.","The transaction becomes invalid or problematic where the bank appoints the Mutawarriq as its own agent to both purchase and then sell back to himself (collapsing the two legs into one interdependent transaction without genuine possession or risk transfer), or where it is conducted via commodity exchanges where the goods never actually move; scholars such as Monzer Kahf and Nejatullah Siddiqi have warned that widespread individual/consumer use of Tawarruq (e.g. personal financing, credit cards) is little more than a device to obtain cash against collateral, and should be restricted to unavoidable corporate liquidity needs."],"commonMistakes":["Treating Tawarruq and Bai' al-'Inah as identical — the book is explicit that the third-party resale is what separates the (mostly permitted) Tawarruq from the (mostly prohibited) 'Inah.","Assuming Tawarruq is problem-free simply because it is technically permissible — the book stresses that extensive or careless (especially agency-based or exchange-based) use undermines its Shari'ah basis and creates systemic risk."],"topicId":"t13.3","chapter":13,"source":{"chapter":13,"section":"13.3","pages":[349,350,351]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}]});
